{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260711-001-r10s0.yaml; git status --short cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260711-001-r10s0.yaml; rg -n 'hyp-20260711-001-r10s0' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260711-001-r10s0.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260711-001-r10s0\ntitle: \"Register-slice the RVFI retirement bundle\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The FPGA benchmark deliberately XOR-reduces every RVFI output into the LED,\n  so the complete retirement payload remains a live, high-fanout timing cone.\n  Today fields from MEM/WB and the combinational writeback mux drive that\n  external reduction directly, even though RVFI consumers only require an\n  ordered stream of architectural retirements rather than zero extra latency.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add one dedicated RVFI output register in core.sv. Capture every channel-0\n  RVFI field and the current order value atomically when MEM/WB retires, then\n  drive the top-level ports solely from that register. The extra fixed\n  observation cycle leaves execution, marker timing, and retirement order\n  unchanged while cutting the MEM/WB/writeback-to-wrapper fanout path. This\n  should improve placed Fmax without extending fetch, ALU, memory, or branch\n  timing cones.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Introduce an atomically captured channel-0 RVFI register, delay valid and all payload fields together by one observation cycle, and preserve contiguous order generation from MEM/WB retirement.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Add retirement-stream checks proving the registered RVFI bundle preserves field coherence, strict order, bubbles, memory masks, and the final EBREAK record despite the fixed output delay.\"\n1:id: hyp-20260711-001-r10s0\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
