{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"git diff --check && sed -n '1,160p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260711-001-r4s0.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260711-001-r4s0\ntitle: \"Single narrow RV32M multiplier\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The ALU currently builds three unconditional 64-by-64 signed-product\n  variants to serve the four RV32M multiply operations. Those oversized,\n  parallel multiplier cones consume substantial LUT and routing resources on\n  every cycle, even though RV32 operands need at most a signed 33-by-33\n  product to produce every required low or high result bit.\nhypothesis: |\n  Select operand extension from the decoded multiply operation and compute one\n  signed 33-by-33 product, selecting its low half for MUL and bits [63:32]\n  for MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU. This preserves all RV32M signedness semantics\n  while eliminating two parallel multipliers and avoiding the accidental\n  64-bit operand expansion. The smaller arithmetic fabric should reduce\n  placement pressure and improve Fmax without adding latency or changing IPC.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: \"Replace the three unconditional 64-bit product expressions with one operation-selected signed 33-by-33 product; retain the exact RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS mappings and all divide/remainder behavior.\"\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: \"Add or retain boundary and randomized vectors for MUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU, including signed-negative and maximal-unsigned operands, to prove the shared product selects the exact RV32M result.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
