enever dmemReady is high. CoreMark has frequent load-fed arithmetic and\\npointer updates, so this compulsory bubble directly lowers iterations per\\ncycle under the zero-wait and randomly stalled evaluation model.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a MEM-to-EX load-result bypass so the instruction directly following a\\nload enters EX while that load is in MEM, consuming the already\\nsign/zero-extended dmem value instead of its address ALU result. Remove the\\nunconditional load-use interlock; when dmemReady is low, the existing\\ndmem-wide pipeline hold keeps both the load and dependent instruction in\\nplace until the data is valid. This changes the forwarding topology without\\nextending the fetch predictor or changing in-order retirement semantics.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Distinguish a writing load in EX/MEM from an ALU producer and select a dedicated same-cycle memory-result forwarding source for matching EX operands.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept the sign/zero-extended MEM load result as the youngest forwarding input for rs1 and rs2, including store-data, branch, and JALR operand uses.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the MEM-stage bypass result into the EX forwarding network while retaining current dmem-stall priority and RVFI wiring.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Eliminate the fixed load-use bubble while preserving global dmem backpressure holds, redirect precedence, and bubbles for no other reason.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add back-to-back load-to-ALU, load-to-store, load-to-branch, and load-to-JALR tests under ready and delayed dmem responses, checking retirement order and RVFI operands.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Distinguish a writing load in EX/MEM from an ALU producer and select a dedicated same-cycle memory-result forwarding source for matching EX operands.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept the sign/zero-extended MEM load result as the youngest forwarding input for rs1 and rs2, including store-data, branch, and JALR operand uses.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the MEM-stage bypass result into the EX forwarding network while retaining current dmem-stall priority and RVFI wiring.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Eliminate the fixed load-use bubble while preserving global dmem backpressure holds, redirect precedence, and bubbles for no other reason.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add back-to-back load-to-ALU, load-to-store, load-to-branch, and load-to-JALR tests under ready and delayed dmem responses, checking retirement order and RVFI operands.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 229.45, \"delta_pct\": -34.19, \"vs_baseline\": -18.87, \"fmax_mhz\": 90.68, \"ipc_coremark\": 3e-06, \"cycles\": 3952044, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 8002, \"ff\": 1936, \"seeds\": [93.77, 87.57, 90.68], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T17:37:04.304869Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 8, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r8s1 (regression, -34.19%): A same-cycle dmem-to-EX load bypass can lose badly when its read-data extension and forwarding mux extend the dependent ALU timing cone, so removing a CoreMark load-use bubble requires post-route proof that the memory-to-EX path is faster than the saved cycle.\"}\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r1s1 (broken, n/a): An iterative EX divider must preserve the pipeline's valid/retirement progress contract while it holds ID/EX; freezing around a multicycle response can make DIV/REM formal assumptions unreachable and fail liveness even when unrelated instructions pass.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r1s2 (improvement, +11.79%): A compact PC-indexed two-bit BHT that predicts direct B-immediate targets and JALs removes the repeated two-slot EX recovery bubbles in CoreMark's short counted loops, provided EX compares resolved and carried predicted next PCs before flushing.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r2s2 (regression, -2.41%): For this CoreMark front end, a committed four-entry RAS's canonical-JALR decode, stack-top target mux, and EX training state cost more than eliminating return recovery bubbles, so return prediction must stay off the fetch-critical path unless its dynamic benefit is demonstrably larger.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r3s1 (improvement, +8.89%): Resolving conditional branches in ID with EX/MEM/WB operand bypasses and a load-use interlock recovers one CoreMark mispredict slot without extending the fetch predictor's Fmax-critical lookup cone, provided the carried early-resolved bit suppresses EX's duplicate redirect.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r3s2 (regression, -2.47%): On this CoreMark front end, adding a per-entry valid-bit read and BTFNT direction mux to the BHT lookup cone costs more than it saves on first-iteration backward-loop redirects, so cold-start policy must not lengthen the fetch-critical predictor path without a larger benefit.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r5s0 (regression, -23.69%): Once branches are already resolved in ID, carrying a 32-bit successor plus direction and misalignment state through ID/EX only trades EX recomputation for wider registered routing without reducing CoreMark recovery bubbles, so duplicate control logic should remain unless post-route timing proves the payload is cheaper.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r5s2 (improvement, +0.31%): When a legal conditional branch is operand-ready in ID, training its two-bit BHT under the same capture/redirect gate removes a cycle of stale counter state for nearby loop branches and aliases without lengthening the fetch lookup cone.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r6s0 (improvement, +0.95%): Sharing a single unsigned magnitude quotient/remainder cone across DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU modestly reduces divider LUT/routing pressure while preserving RV32M sign, zero-divisor, and INT_MIN/-1 behavior through explicit result restoration.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r6s2 (regression, -1.90%): With a two-bit BHT already in the fetch path, weakly-taken reset harms CoreMark more through cold forward/not-taken branches and aliases than it saves on first counted-loop back edges, so the weakly-not-taken cold bias is the better default.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r7s2 (regression, -8.26%): Decode-resolving canonical JALR returns with bypass readiness, load-dependency, and alignment checks regresses CoreMark when the saved recovery slot on short helper returns cannot amortize the added ID-stage control/target logic, so return recovery needs a lower-cost path or stronger dynamic evidence.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r8s0 (regression, -46.02%): Replacing signed MULH/MULHSU product cones with unsigned-high-half sign corrections can severely hurt the ALU critical path because the multiplier result now feeds conditional 32-bit subtractor chains with sign/operand fanout, so arithmetic sharing must be judged post-route rather than by multiplier-cone count.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r8s1 (regression, -34.19%): A same-cycle dmem-to-EX load bypass can lose badly when its read-data extension and forwarding mux extend the dependent ALU timing cone, so removing a CoreMark load-use bubble requires post-route proof that the memory-to-EX path is faster than the saved cycle.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r8s2 (improvement, +1.43%): After operand-ready conditional branches redirect in ID, removing the BHT's counter lookup, branch-legality decode, target adder, and direction mux from the fetch-to-PC path can outperform its remaining load-dependent-branch benefit while retaining immediate direct-JAL prediction.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"jq -r 'select(.category == \\\"structural\\\") | [.id, .title, .outcome, (.fitness // \\\"-\\\"), (.delta_pct // \\\"-\\\")] | @tsv' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl && rg -n 'hyp-20260711-002-r(4|5|6)s1' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"jq -r 'select(.category == \\\"structural\\\") | [.id, .title, .outcome, (.fitness // \\\"-\\\"), (.delta_pct // \\\"-\\\")] | @tsv' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl && rg -n 'hyp-20260711-002-r(4|5|6)s1' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"hyp-20260711-002-r1s1\tIterative divide execution unit\tbroken\t-\t-\nhyp-20260711-002-r2s1\tBounded radix-4 divide pipeline\tbroken\t-\t-\nhyp-20260711-002-r3s1\tDecode-stage branch resolution\timprovement\t344.27\t8.89\nhyp-20260711-002-r4s1\tTwo-entry instruction skid queue\tregression\t334.25\t-2.91\nhyp-20260711-002-r5s1\tOne-entry decoupled store buffer\tplacement_failed\t-\t-\nhyp-20260711-002-r6s1\tDecode-stage JALR recovery\tbroken\t-\t-\nhyp-20260711-002-r7s1\tForwarded decode-stage JALR recovery\tbroken\t-\t-\nhyp-20260711-002-r8s1\tZero-bubble load result bypass\tregression\t229.45\t-34.19\n12:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r4s1\", \"title\": \"Two-entry instruction skid queue\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The CoreMark cosim injects roughly 22% instruction-bus backpressure, and\\nthe current front end turns every unready imem cycle into a full fetch and\\ndecode bubble. The successful branch work has already reduced redirect\\nwaste, so absorbing independent delivery stalls is now a larger structural\\nsource of lost issue slots.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The CoreMark cosim injects roughly 22% instruction-bus backpressure, and\\nthe current front end turns every unready imem cycle into a full fetch and\\ndecode bubble. The successful branch work has already reduced redirect\\nwaste, so absorbing independent delivery stalls is now a larger structural\\nsource of lost issue slots.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the combinational IF-to-ID payload with a two-entry, PC-tagged\\ninstruction skid queue. Fetch fills an empty queue slot whenever imem is\\nready while ID independently consumes the head, allowing a previously\\nfetched instruction to enter decode during a later imem stall. Each entry\\ncarries its predictor-selected successor so sequential prefetch remains\\ncorrect; EX and ID redirects synchronously invalidate younger queued\\nentries and restart fetch at the resolved target. This keeps the existing\\nsingle-retire pipeline and RVFI boundary intact while converting many\\nisolated zero-wait misses into no-bubble cycles.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 7, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a fetch-queue entry bundle containing PC, instruction, predicted successor, and validity metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the combinational IF/ID payload with a two-entry instruction skid queue, imem-ready fill control, and redirect-safe invalidation/restart logic.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire imem readiness, decode-consume control, and the existing ID/EX redirect priorities into the decoupled fetch front end.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Change instruction-stall control from immediate pipeline bubbling to queue-head availability and preserve precise load-use, dmem-stall, and redirect precedence.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose the decode-stage consume/hold condition required to pop exactly one queued instruction only when ID/EX can capture it.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed randomized-imem-stall tests for queue fill/drain, branch and JAL redirect flushing, load-use holds, RVFI order, and no duplicate retirement.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 7, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a fetch-queue entry bundle containing PC, instruction, predicted successor, and validity metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the combinational IF/ID payload with a two-entry instruction skid queue, imem-ready fill control, and redirect-safe invalidation/restart logic.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire imem readiness, decode-consume control, and the existing ID/EX redirect priorities into the decoupled fetch front end.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Change instruction-stall control from immediate pipeline bubbling to queue-head availability and preserve precise load-use, dmem-stall, and redirect precedence.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose the decode-stage consume/hold condition required to pop exactly one queued instruction only when ID/EX can capture it.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed randomized-imem-stall tests for queue fill/drain, branch and JAL redirect flushing, load-use holds, RVFI order, and no duplicate retirement.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 334.25, \"delta_pct\": -2.91, \"vs_baseline\": 18.18, \"fmax_mhz\": 131.65, \"ipc_coremark\": 3e-06, \"cycles\": 3938669, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 11431, \"ff\": 2123, \"seeds\": [131.13, 131.65, 137.1], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T14:11:15.845885Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 4}\n15:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"One-entry decoupled store buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Random dmem backpressure currently freezes every pipeline stage whenever a\\nstore reaches EX/MEM, even though a completed store has no register result\\nand most following CoreMark instructions are independent. The failed fetch\\nqueue showed that broad front-end buffering costs too much Fmax, so the\\nnext decoupling point should be off the critical fetch and execute paths.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Random dmem backpressure currently freezes every pipeline stage whenever a\\nstore reaches EX/MEM, even though a completed store has no register result\\nand most following CoreMark instructions are independent. The failed fetch\\nqueue showed that broad front-end buffering costs too much Fmax, so the\\nnext decoupling point should be off the critical fetch and execute paths.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Insert a one-entry write buffer between MEM and the dmem pins. A store may\\nretire into an empty buffer in one cycle even when dmemReady is low; the\\nbuffer holds its aligned address, replicated data, and byte enables until\\nthe bus accepts it. Loads and a second store wait until the older buffered\\nstore drains, preserving in-order memory visibility, while independent\\nALU/control instructions continue through the existing single-retire\\npipeline. This converts isolated store-side bus stalls into useful work\\nwithout lengthening the predictor lookup or EX arithmetic cone.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/store_buffer.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a one-entry valid/ready write buffer that captures an aligned store transaction, drives dmem until accepted, and exposes empty/drain state.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Route stores through the buffer, permit an empty-buffer store to advance without dmemReady, and retain exact RVFI store address, mask, and data fields at retirement.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace unconditional store-side dmem freezes with buffer-full and ordered-load interlocks while retaining the existing load and redirect priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire store enqueue, buffer drain, dmem pins, and memory-order status through the existing MEM and hazard controls.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add randomized dmem-stall cases proving independent instructions advance behind a buffered store, younger loads and stores wait for drain, and RVFI/order/memory data remain exact.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/store_buffer.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a one-entry valid/ready write buffer that captures an aligned store transaction, drives dmem until accepted, and exposes empty/drain state.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Route stores through the buffer, permit an empty-buffer store to advance without dmemReady, and retain exact RVFI store address, mask, and data fields at retirement.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace unconditional store-side dmem freezes with buffer-full and ordered-load interlocks while retaining the existing load and redirect priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire store enqueue, buffer drain, dmem pins, and memory-order status through the existing MEM and hazard controls.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add randomized dmem-stall cases proving independent instructions advance behind a buffered store, younger loads and stores wait for drain, and RVFI/order/memory data remain exact.\"}], \"outcome\": \"placement_failed\", \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": \"placement_failed\", \"seeds\": [null, null, null], \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 5}\n18:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r6s1\", \"title\": \"Decode-stage JALR recovery\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Conditional branches already recover in decode, but every JALR still\\nreaches EX before the core discovers that sequential fetch is wrong. CoreMark\\nuses call and return control flow often enough that this leaves a repeated\\ntwo-slot recovery penalty after the BHT has removed most loop-branch waste.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Conditional branches already recover in decode, but every JALR still\\nreaches EX before the core discovers that sequential fetch is wrong. CoreMark\\nuses call and return control flow often enough that this leaves a repeated\\ntwo-slot recovery penalty after the BHT has removed most loop-branch waste.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Extend the existing decode-stage control-resolution path to legal JALR\\ninstructions. Reuse its EX/MEM/WB operand bypasses to calculate the masked\\nrs1+immediate target in ID and redirect one cycle before EX whenever the\\nsource is ready and the target is aligned. Carry a single generalized\\ncontrol-resolved bit into EX to suppress duplicate recovery; load-dependent\\nor misaligned JALRs retain the current EX path so their interlock and trap\\nsemantics stay exact. This changes recovery-stage topology without adding a\\ntarget lookup or return stack to the fetch-critical predictor cone.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Generalize the existing ID/EX branch-resolved marker into a control-resolved-in-ID bit without widening the carried successor payload.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Resolve legal, operand-ready, aligned JALR targets with the existing decode bypass network and emit the existing early redirect interface for both branches and JALR.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Preserve JALR link-value and misalignment handling while suppressing EX recovery for a control transfer already redirected in decode.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the generalized decode control redirect through the existing fetch and redirect-priority paths without changing the predictor lookup cone.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Retain load-use and dmem-stall precedence while treating a decode-resolved JALR exactly like the existing one-slot decode branch recovery.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add independent, ALU-forwarded, load-dependent, and mis