: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_6-terra-rep3/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20260711-003-r10s2/formal/last_run-50634.log\\nPer-check logs in: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_6-terra-rep3/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20260711-003-r10s2/formal/riscv-formal/cores/bench-50634/checks/<check>/logfile.txt\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 10}\n32:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r11s0\", \"title\": \"Prune duplicate decode writeback bypass\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"Conditional branches now resolve in ID, so their bypass muxes participate in\\nthe decode-to-PC recovery cone. The register file is explicitly write-first,\\nbut ID repeats a writeback address comparison and data mux for each branch\\nsource before applying the genuinely necessary MEM and EX bypasses.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Conditional branches now resolve in ID, so their bypass muxes participate in\\nthe decode-to-PC recovery cone. The register file is explicitly write-first,\\nbut ID repeats a writeback address comparison and data mux for each branch\\nsource before applying the genuinely necessary MEM and EX bypasses.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Remove ID's redundant WB-to-branch operand bypass and its three writeback\\nports. The regfile already returns wb_w_data for a same-cycle matching read\\naddress, making branch_rs1/branch_rs2 identical before the remaining\\nage-prioritized MEM and EX overrides. This eliminates two 5-bit comparators,\\nzero-register tests, and 32-bit mux legs from the early branch-resolution\\ncone, improving Fmax without changing instruction scheduling, IPC, RVFI, or\\nbypass semantics.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Delete the redundant writeback bypass inputs and comparison/mux legs from decode-stage branch operand selection, retaining MEM and EX forwarding priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the now-unused writeback-to-ID branch-bypass wiring while retaining the register-file write port and all other pipeline connections.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add a branch whose source register is written in WB during decode, plus EX and MEM override cases, to prove write-first regfile behavior and branch RVFI sequencing remain exact.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Delete the redundant writeback bypass inputs and comparison/mux legs from decode-stage branch operand selection, retaining MEM and EX forwarding priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the now-unused writeback-to-ID branch-bypass wiring while retaining the register-file write port and all other pipeline connections.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add a branch whose source register is written in WB during decode, plus EX and MEM override cases, to prove write-first regfile behavior and branch RVFI sequencing remain exact.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 333.04, \"delta_pct\": -5.81, \"vs_baseline\": 17.76, \"fmax_mhz\": 138.16, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4148503, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 7863, \"ff\": 1896, \"seeds\": [142.71, 138.16, 134.34], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T18:09:47.303217Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 11}\n33:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r11s1\", \"title\": \"Correct 30-bit word-PC spine\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The fetch PC, predicted successor, and carried retirement PCs are always\\ninstruction aligned, yet the core routes their invariant low two zero bits\\nand uses 32-bit adders in the timing-sensitive fetch and redirect paths.\\nThe prior word-PC attempt failed cosimulation because it retained an\\nencoded halfword immediate bit when converting direct-control offsets, not\\nbecause the reduced-width PC topology is architecturally invalid.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The fetch PC, predicted successor, and carried retirement PCs are always\\ninstruction aligned, yet the core routes their invariant low two zero bits\\nand uses 32-bit adders in the timing-sensitive fetch and redirect paths.\\nThe prior word-PC attempt failed cosimulation because it retained an\\nencoded halfword immediate bit when converting direct-control offsets, not\\nbecause the reduced-width PC topology is architecturally invalid.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Represent sequential fetch, direct-JAL prediction, conditional-branch\\nrecovery, and non-JALR resolved successors as 30-bit instruction-word\\naddresses. PC+4 becomes pc_word+1. Direct JAL must use exactly the signed\\nJ-immediate bits [31:2], and B targets exactly the signed B-immediate bits\\n[31:2], dropping both encoded invariant low bits; this corrects the failed\\nearlier conversion. Reconstruct byte PCs only at imem, AUIPC/link-data,\\nJALR's full-width target/alignment check, and RVFI boundaries. Narrowing\\nthe persistent PC feedback/routing cone should improve Fmax without adding\\nqueueing state or changing single-retirement behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace carried aligned PC and predicted-successor fields with 30-bit word-PC fields while retaining byte-address reconstruction at architectural boundaries.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Store the fetch PC in word units, emit io_imemAddr as {pc_word,2'b00}, and retain redirect-over-stall priority with pc_word+1 sequential advance.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Perform direct-JAL prediction in word units using the exact signed J-immediate [31:2] encoding, with no retained halfword bit.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute decode-resolved conditional branch targets from the exact signed B-immediate [31:2] word displacement while preserving existing operand bypass and trap gating.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use word-PC arithmetic for branch/JAL successors and byte-PC reconstruction for AUIPC/link data; keep JALR full-width until its mandated alignment check completes.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Propagate narrowed PC metadata through MEM/WB and materialize byte-addressed RVFI PC values without altering memory-access semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Adapt predictor, redirect, and RVFI wiring to the internal word-PC representation while preserving the external imem and single-channel RVFI contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add forward/backward JAL and branch cases that verify exact word-offset conversion, plus AUIPC, JALR, misalignment, RVFI PC, and retirement-order coverage.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace carried aligned PC and predicted-successor fields with 30-bit word-PC fields while retaining byte-address reconstruction at architectural boundaries.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Store the fetch PC in word units, emit io_imemAddr as {pc_word,2'b00}, and retain redirect-over-stall priority with pc_word+1 sequential advance.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Perform direct-JAL prediction in word units using the exact signed J-immediate [31:2] encoding, with no retained halfword bit.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute decode-resolved conditional branch targets from the exact signed B-immediate [31:2] word displacement while preserving existing operand bypass and trap gating.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use word-PC arithmetic for branch/JAL successors and byte-PC reconstruction for AUIPC/link data; keep JALR full-width until its mandated alignment check completes.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Propagate narrowed PC metadata through MEM/WB and materialize byte-addressed RVFI PC values without altering memory-access semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Adapt predictor, redirect, and RVFI wiring to the internal word-PC representation while preserving the external imem and single-channel RVFI contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add forward/backward JAL and branch cases that verify exact word-offset conversion, plus AUIPC, JALR, misalignment, RVFI PC, and retirement-order coverage.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 140.48, \"delta_pct\": -60.27, \"vs_baseline\": -50.33, \"fmax_mhz\": 58.28, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4148503, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 7923, \"ff\": 1906, \"seeds\": [58.1, 58.28, 61.98], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T18:13:43.138227Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 11}\n34:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-003-r11s2\", \"title\": \"Aligned decode-stage JAL steering\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current JAL-only predictor avoids direct-JAL recovery bubbles, but it\\nstill places J-immediate extraction and a 32-bit PC-relative target adder\\non every fetch-to-PC feedback path. Conditional branches already redirect\\nfrom decode without a bubble when their result is safe to capture.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current JAL-only predictor avoids direct-JAL recovery bubbles, but it\\nstill places J-immediate extraction and a 32-bit PC-relative target adder\\non every fetch-to-PC feedback path. Conditional branches already redirect\\nfrom decode without a bubble when their result is safe to capture.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace only the fetch-stage direct-JAL target prediction with a verified\\ndecode-stage JAL redirect. Fetch remains sequential, while a legal JAL\\nwhose target is instruction aligned steers the PC on the same edge that\\ncaptures it into ID/EX. Carry a one-bit captured-resolution marker so EX\\nrecomputes the architectural successor for RVFI but never performs a\\nduplicate redirect. Misaligned JAL targets, older EX recovery, stalls, and\\nflushes retain their existing EX-stage behavior. This removes the JAL\\nimmediate adder and selection mux from the Fmax-critical fetch loop without\\nadding return prediction, branch-table state, or decode operand bypassing.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Reduce lookup to the sequential successor so no J-immediate extraction or target adder remains on the fetch prediction path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a direct-JAL decode redirect only when the instruction is valid, legal, aligned, capturable, and not superseded by the existing flush or stall controls; set a resolution marker only under that same gate.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Generalize the ID/EX decode-resolution marker from conditional branches to aligned decode-resolved direct control flow without widening the carried successor payload.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Keep full JAL target and misalignment computation for architectural pc_next and traps, but suppress recovery solely for the captured decode-resolved marker.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the sequential predictor and decode JAL redirect through the existing older-EX-wins redirect priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add aligned forward and backward JAL steering tests plus stall, older-redirect, and misaligned-JAL cases that check retirement order, RVFI PCs, link writes, and trap behavior.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Reduce lookup to the sequential successor so no J-immediate extraction or target adder remains on the fetch prediction path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a direct-JAL decode redirect only when the instruction is valid, legal, aligned, capturable, and not superseded by the existing flush or stall controls; set a resolution marker only under that same gate.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Generalize the ID/EX decode-resolution marker from conditional branches to aligned decode-resolved direct control flow without widening the carried successor payload.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Keep full JAL target and misalignment computation for architectural pc_next and traps, but suppress recovery solely for the captured decode-resolved marker.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the sequential predictor and decode JAL redirect through the existing older-EX-wins redirect priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add aligned forward and backward JAL steering tests plus stall, older-redirect, and misaligned-JAL cases that check retirement order, RVFI PCs, link writes, and trap behavior.\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 380.62, \"delta_pct\": 7.64, \"vs_baseline\": 34.58, \"fmax_mhz\": 157.9, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4148503, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 7729, \"ff\": 1910, \"seeds\": [165.4, 135.81, 157.9], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T18:16:23.476499Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 11, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r11s2 (improvement, +7.64%): Once ID can capture a legal aligned JAL and carry a resolution marker to suppress EX recovery, moving its immediate target adder out of the fetch-to-PC loop improves CoreMark timing without sacrificing architectural successor or misalignment handling.\"}\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r1s1 (broken, n/a): An iterative EX divider must preserve the pipeline's valid/retirement progress contract while it holds ID/EX; freezing around a multicycle response can make DIV/REM formal assumptions unreachable and fail liveness even when unrelated instructions pass.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r1s2 (improvement, +11.79%): A compact PC-indexed two-bit BHT that predicts direct B-immediate targets and JALs removes the repeated two-slot EX recovery bubbles in CoreMark's short counted loops, provided EX compares resolved and carried predicted next PCs before flushing.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r2s2 (regression, -2.41%): For this CoreMark front end, a committed four-entry RAS's canonical-JALR decode, stack-top target mux, and EX training state cost more than eliminating return recovery bubbles, so return prediction must stay off the fetch-critical path unless its dynamic benefit is demonstrably larger.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r3s1 (improvement, +8.89%): Resolving conditional branches in ID with EX/MEM/WB operand bypasses and a load-use interlock recovers one CoreMark mispredict slot without extending the fetch predictor's Fmax-critical lookup cone, provided the carried early-resolved bit suppresses EX's duplicate redirect.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r3s2 (regression, -2.47%): On this CoreMark front end, adding a per-entry valid-bit read and BTFNT direction mux to the BHT lookup cone costs more than it saves on first-iteration backward-loop redirects, so cold-start policy must not lengthen the fetch-critical predictor path without a larger benefit.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r5s0 (regression, -23.69%): Once branches are already resolved in ID, carrying a 32-bit successor plus direction and misalignment state through ID/EX only trades EX recomputation for wider registered routing without reducing CoreMark recovery bubbles, so duplicate control logic should remain unless post-route timing proves the payload is cheaper.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r5s2 (improvement, +0.31%): When a legal conditional branch is operand-ready in ID, training its two-bit BHT under the same capture/redirect gate removes a cycle of stale counter state for nearby loop branches and aliases without lengthening the fetch lookup cone.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r6s0 (improvement, +0.95%): Sharing a single unsigned magnitude quotient/remainder cone across DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU modestly reduces divider LUT/routing pressure while preserving RV32M sign, zero-divisor, and INT_MIN/-1 behavior through explicit result restoration.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r6s2 (regression, -1.90%): With a two-bit BHT already in the fetch path, weakly-taken reset harms CoreMark more through cold forward/not-taken branches and aliases than it saves on first counted-loop back edges, so the weakly-not-taken cold bias is the better default.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r7s2 (regression, -8.26%): Decode-resolving canonical JALR returns with bypass readiness, load-dependency, and alignment checks regresses CoreMark when the saved recovery slot on short helper returns cannot amortize the added ID-stage control/target logic, so return recovery needs a lower-cost path or stronger dynamic evidence.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r8s0 (regression, -46.02%): Replacing signed MULH/MULHSU product cones with unsigned-high-half sign corrections can severely hurt the ALU critical path because the multiplier result now feeds conditional 32-bit subtractor chains with sign/operand fanout, so arithmetic sharing must be judged post-route rather than by multiplier-cone count.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r8s1 (regression, -34.19%): A same-cycle dmem-to-EX load bypass can lose badly when its read-data extension and forwarding mux extend the dependent ALU timing cone, so removing a CoreMark load-use bubble requires post-route proof that the memory-to-EX path is faster than the saved cycle.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r8s2 (improvement, +1.43%): After operand-ready conditional branches redirect in ID, removing the BHT's counter lookup, branch-legality decode, target adder, and direction mux from the fetch-to-PC path can outperform its remaining load-dependent-branch benefit while retaining immediate direct-JAL prediction.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r9s0 (regression, -61.98%): Splitting a live FPGA-visible 64-bit counter can regress badly when the low-word terminal-count compare and conditional high-word increment add a wide control path that outweighs the shorter common carry chain, so counter decomposition needs post-route evidence rather than carry-width intuition.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r9s2 (regression, -20.97%): Even a bypass-free `jalr x0, x1, 0` decode redirect regresses CoreMark when x1-pending comparisons, target alignment gating, and the ID early-redirect selection add control/routing cost that common return bubbles cannot amortize, so canonical-return recovery should remain in EX unless its decode path is demonstrably cheaper post-route.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r10s0 (regression, -58.18%): Registering the entire RVFI retirement payload can lose badly on this FPGA because the added wide bundle of flip-flops and routing outweighs any relief from removing MEM/WB and writeback-mux fanout, so high-fanout observability logic needs post-route validation before adding a full-width pipeline stage.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r10s1 (broken, n/a): Word-PC control flow must convert JAL/B immediates from byte to word units by dropping both invariant low bits; retaining the encoded halfword bit scales even legal aligned targets incorrectly and causes cosimulation failures.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r11s2 (improvement, +7.64%): Once ID can capture a legal aligned JAL and carry a resolution marker to suppress EX recovery, moving its immediate target adder out of the fetch-to-PC loop improves CoreMark timing without sacrificing architectural successor or misalignment handling.\n// rtl/branch_predictor.sv\n//\n// Fetch-stage successor selector. Every instruction advances sequentially;\n// conditional branches and aligned direct JALs redirect from ID, while JALR\n// and all unresolved or misaligned control flow recover in EX. Keeping this\n// logic to a single increment removes J-immediate extraction and the\n// PC-relative target adder from the fetch-to-PC feedback loop.\nmodule branch_predictor (\n  input  logic [31:0] lookup_pc,\n  output logic [31:0] predicted_next_pc\n);\n\n  always_comb begin\n    predicted_next_pc = lookup_pc + 32'd4;\n  end\n\nendmodule\ncores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv:39:  output logic              early_redirect,\ncores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv:40:  output logic [31:0]       early_redirect_target,\ncores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv:127:  logic        control_resolved_id;\ncores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv:193:    control_resolved_id = branch_resolved_id || jal_resolved_id;\ncores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv:195:    early_redirect_target = control_next_pc;\ncores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv:196:    early_redirect = control_resolved_id\ncores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv:197:                  && (control_next_pc != in.predicted_next_pc);\ncores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv:213:      reg_q.predicted_next_pc <= in.predicted_next_pc;\ncores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv:214:      reg_q.control_resolved_id <= control_resolved_id;\ncores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv:27:  input  logic [31:0]       predicted_next_pc,\ncores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv:43:                          : predicted_next_pc;\ncores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv:67:    out.predicted_next_pc = predicted_next_pc;\ncores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv:81:    logic [31:0] predicted_next_pc;\ncores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv:91:    logic [31:0] predicted_next_pc;\ncores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv:95:    logic        control_resolved_id;\ncores/bench/rtl/core.sv:95:  logic [31:0] predicted_next_pc;\ncores/bench/rtl/core.sv:97:  branch_predictor u_predictor (\ncores/bench/rtl/core.sv:99:    .predicted_next_pc (predicted_next_pc)\ncores/bench/rtl/core.sv:121:    .predicted_next_pc(predicted_next_pc),\ncores/bench/rtl/core.sv:147:    .early_redirect(id_redirect),\ncores/bench/rtl/core.sv:148:    .early_redirect_target(id_redirect_target),\ncores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv:139:  assign redirect        = in.valid && !stall && !in.control_resolved_id\ncores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv:140:                         && (resolved_next_pc != in.predicted_next_pc);\ncores/bench/rtl/branch_predictor.sv:1:// rtl/branch_predictor.sv\ncores/bench/rtl/branch_predictor.sv:8:module branch_predictor (\ncores/bench/rtl/branch_predictor.sv:10:  output logic [31:0] predicted_next_pc\ncores/bench/rtl/branch_predictor.sv:14:    predicted_next_pc = lookup_pc + 32'd4;\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
