c=0)\\\\n\\\\nFull run log: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_6-terra-rep3/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20260711-002-r7s1/formal/last_run-57416.log\\\\nPer-check logs in: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_6-terra-rep3/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20260711-002-r7s1/formal/riscv-formal/cores/bench-57416/checks/<check>/logfile.txt\\\", \\\"slot\\\": 1, \\\"round_id\\\": 7}\\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:22:{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260711-003-r7s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Decode-assisted return recovery\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Direct branches and JALs already avoid most front-end recovery, but canonical\\\\nfunction returns still default to the sequential path until EX. CoreMark's\\\\nsmall helper routines make this a recurring remaining control-flow penalty.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Direct branches and JALs already avoid most front-end recovery, but canonical\\\\nfunction returns still default to the sequential path until EX. CoreMark's\\\\nsmall helper routines make this a recurring remaining control-flow penalty.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Recognize only canonical returns (JALR x0, x1, 0) in decode and use the\\\\nexisting EX/MEM/WB bypass network to form the aligned x1 target there. When\\\\nx1 is ready, redirect fetch one cycle earlier and mark the control transfer\\\\nresolved so EX preserves its normal RVFI pc_wdata without issuing a duplicate\\\\nredirect. This is a return-specific late predictor: unlike the prior RAS, it\\\\nadds no state, tag, or target mux to the fetch-critical BHT lookup cone; unlike\\\\nthe broad JALR experiment, it leaves calls and arbitrary indirect jumps on the\\\\nproven EX path. Load-dependent or misaligned returns retain EX resolution and\\\\nexact trap behavior.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Generalize the ID/EX branch-resolved marker to denote a control transfer resolved in decode, without carrying a successor payload.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Detect legal canonical returns, reuse the established operand bypass/readiness checks to calculate the masked aligned target, and emit a decode redirect only for a capturable non-faulting return.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Honor the generalized decode-resolution marker for canonical returns while retaining the existing JALR link, target, and misalignment-trap calculations for retirement correctness.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Wire the decode return-recovery request through the existing older-EX redirect priority and fetch-control interfaces without changing branch_predictor lookup logic.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_pipeline.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add canonical-return cases for register-file, ALU-forwarded, load-dependent, and misaligned targets; check one-cycle recovery, RVFI PC/order, and that noncanonical JALRs still use EX recovery.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Generalize the ID/EX branch-resolved marker to denote a control transfer resolved in decode, without carrying a successor payload.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Detect legal canonical returns, reuse the established operand bypass/readiness checks to calculate the masked aligned target, and emit a decode redirect only for a capturable non-faulting return.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Honor the generalized decode-resolution marker for canonical returns while retaining the existing JALR link, target, and misalignment-trap calculations for retirement correctness.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Wire the decode return-recovery request through the existing older-EX redirect priority and fetch-control interfaces without changing branch_predictor lookup logic.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_pipeline.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add canonical-return cases for register-file, ALU-forwarded, load-dependent, and misaligned targets; check one-cycle recovery, RVFI PC/order, and that noncanonical JALRs still use EX recovery.\\\"}], \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"regression\\\", \\\"fitness\\\": 319.82, \\\"delta_pct\\\": -8.26, \\\"vs_baseline\\\": 13.08, \\\"fmax_mhz\\\": 132.28, \\\"ipc_coremark\\\": 2e-06, \\\"cycles\\\": 4136096, \\\"iterations\\\": 10, \\\"lut4\\\": 8025, \\\"ff\\\": 1926, \\\"seeds\\\": [132.28, 131.84, 155.88], \\\"formal_passed\\\": true, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": true, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"implementation_notes\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-07-11T17:10:21.056667Z\\\", \\\"slot\\\": 2, \\\"round_id\\\": 7, \\\"lesson\\\": \\\"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r7s2 (regression, -8.26%): Decode-resolving canonical JALR returns with bypass readiness, load-dependency, and alignment checks regresses CoreMark when the saved recovery slot on short helper returns cannot amortize the added ID-stage control/target logic, so return recovery needs a lower-cost path or stronger dynamic evidence.\\\"}\\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:23:{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260711-001-r8s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Unsigned product high-half correction\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current ALU materializes three unconditional multiplier variants even\\\\nthough all four RV32M multiply operations share the same unsigned 32 by 32\\\\npartial product. The prior signed 33-bit shared multiplier regressed, so a\\\\nsmaller correction-based reuse of the already efficient unsigned product is\\\\nthe safer remaining arithmetic optimization.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current ALU materializes three unconditional multiplier variants even\\\\nthough all four RV32M multiply operations share the same unsigned 32 by 32\\\\npartial product. The prior signed 33-bit shared multiplier regressed, so a\\\\nsmaller correction-based reuse of the already efficient unsigned product is\\\\nthe safer remaining arithmetic optimization.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep one unsigned 32 by 32 product for MUL and MULHU, then derive MULH and\\\\nMULHSU high halves from that product with modulo-2^32 sign corrections:\\\\nsubtract b when rs1 is negative and subtract a when rs2 is negative, with\\\\nonly the applicable term used for MULHSU. This removes the two wide signed\\\\nproduct cones without changing EX latency, forwarding, retirement, or the\\\\nproven divide datapath. It should reduce LUT/routing pressure and raise Fmax\\\\nwhile preserving CoreMark IPC.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace mul_ss and mul_su with high-half sign-correction logic driven from the existing unsigned 32 by 32 product; preserve all RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS mappings and divide/remainder behavior.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_alu.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add exact boundary and randomized MULH/MULHSU vectors covering each operand sign combination, including INT_MIN and all-ones unsigned operands.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace mul_ss and mul_su with high-half sign-correction logic driven from the existing unsigned 32 by 32 product; preserve all RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS mappings and divide/remainder behavior.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_alu.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add exact boundary and randomized MULH/MULHSU vectors covering each operand sign combination, including INT_MIN and all-ones unsigned operands.\\\"}], \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"regression\\\", \\\"fitness\\\": 188.19, \\\"delta_pct\\\": -46.02, \\\"vs_baseline\\\": -33.46, \\\"fmax_mhz\\\": 78.07, \\\"ipc_coremark\\\": 2e-06, \\\"cycles\\\": 4148503, \\\"iterations\\\": 10, \\\"lut4\\\": 8166, \\\"ff\\\": 1931, \\\"seeds\\\": [78.47, 78.07, 73.76], \\\"formal_passed\\\": true, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": true, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"implementation_notes\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-07-11T17:29:10.151064Z\\\", \\\"slot\\\": 0, \\\"round_id\\\": 8, \\\"lesson\\\": \\\"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r8s0 (regression, -46.02%): Replacing signed MULH/MULHSU product cones with unsigned-high-half sign corrections can severely hurt the ALU critical path because the multiplier result now feeds conditional 32-bit subtractor chains with sign/operand fanout, so arithmetic sharing must be judged post-route rather than by multiplier-cone count.\\\"}\\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:24:{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260711-002-r8s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Zero-bubble load result bypass\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current five-stage pipeline always inserts a load-use bubble even\\\\nthough the benchmark memory interface returns read data combinationally\\\\nwhenever dmemReady is high. CoreMark has frequent load-fed arithmetic and\\\\npointer updates, so this compulsory bubble directly lowers iterations per\\\\ncycle under the zero-wait and randomly stalled evaluation model.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current five-stage pipeline always inserts a load-use bubble even\\\\nthough the benchmark memory interface returns read data combinationally\\\\nwhenever dmemReady is high. CoreMark has frequent load-fed arithmetic and\\\\npointer updates, so this compulsory bubble directly lowers iterations per\\\\ncycle under the zero-wait and randomly stalled evaluation model.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a MEM-to-EX load-result bypass so the instruction directly following a\\\\nload enters EX while that load is in MEM, consuming the already\\\\nsign/zero-extended dmem value instead of its address ALU result. Remove the\\\\nunconditional load-use interlock; when dmemReady is low, the existing\\\\ndmem-wide pipeline hold keeps both the load and dependent instruction in\\\\nplace until the data is valid. This changes the forwarding topology without\\\\nextending the fetch predictor or changing in-order retirement semantics.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/forward_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Distinguish a writing load in EX/MEM from an ALU producer and select a dedicated same-cycle memory-result forwarding source for matching EX operands.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Accept the sign/zero-extended MEM load result as the youngest forwarding input for rs1 and rs2, including store-data, branch, and JALR operand uses.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Wire the MEM-stage bypass result into the EX forwarding network while retaining current dmem-stall priority and RVFI wiring.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Eliminate the fixed load-use bubble while preserving global dmem backpressure holds, redirect precedence, and bubbles for no other reason.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_pipeline.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add back-to-back load-to-ALU, load-to-store, load-to-branch, and load-to-JALR tests under ready and delayed dmem responses, checking retirement order and RVFI operands.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/forward_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Distinguish a writing load in EX/MEM from an ALU producer and select a dedicated same-cycle memory-result forwarding source for matching EX operands.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Accept the sign/zero-extended MEM load result as the youngest forwarding input for rs1 and rs2, including store-data, branch, and JALR operand uses.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Wire the MEM-stage bypass result into the EX forwarding network while retaining current dmem-stall priority and RVFI wiring.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Eliminate the fixed load-use bubble while preserving global dmem backpressure holds, redirect precedence, and bubbles for no other reason.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_pipeline.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add back-to-back load-to-ALU, load-to-store, load-to-branch, and load-to-JALR tests under ready and delayed dmem responses, checking retirement order and RVFI operands.\\\"}], \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"regression\\\", \\\"fitness\\\": 229.45, \\\"delta_pct\\\": -34.19, \\\"vs_baseline\\\": -18.87, \\\"fmax_mhz\\\": 90.68, \\\"ipc_coremark\\\": 3e-06, \\\"cycles\\\": 3952044, \\\"iterations\\\": 10, \\\"lut4\\\": 8002, \\\"ff\\\": 1936, \\\"seeds\\\": [93.77, 87.57, 90.68], \\\"formal_passed\\\": true, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": true, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"implementation_notes\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-07-11T17:37:04.304869Z\\\", \\\"slot\\\": 1, \\\"round_id\\\": 8, \\\"lesson\\\": \\\"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r8s1 (regression, -34.19%): A same-cycle dmem-to-EX load bypass can lose badly when its read-data extension and forwarding mux extend the dependent ALU timing cone, so removing a CoreMark load-use bubble requires post-route proof that the memory-to-EX path is faster than the saved cycle.\\\"}\\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:25:{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260711-003-r8s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"JAL-only fetch predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Conditional branches now resolve in ID with bypassing, so a sequential\\\\nconditional fetch can usually be repaired at the same edge without an\\\\nextra decode bubble. The BHT still places a counter-array mux, branch\\\\nlegality decode, immediate-target adder, and direction mux on every\\\\ninstruction-fetch path. Direct JALs cannot use the ID branch mechanism\\\\nand remain worth predicting at fetch.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Conditional branches now resolve in ID with bypassing, so a sequential\\\\nconditional fetch can usually be repaired at the same edge without an\\\\nextra decode bubble. The BHT still places a counter-array mux, branch\\\\nlegality decode, immediate-target adder, and direction mux on every\\\\ninstruction-fetch path. Direct JALs cannot use the ID branch mechanism\\\\nand remain worth predicting at fetch.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the conditional two-bit BHT prediction with a minimal direct-JAL\\\\nfetch predictor: JAL is still sent immediately to its encoded target, but\\\\nconditional branches always fetch PC+4 and rely on the established\\\\noperand-ready ID redirect for taken outcomes. Remove BHT state and its\\\\ndecode-stage training interface. This trades the limited remaining benefit\\\\nof speculative conditional direction (principally load-dependent branches)\\\\nfor a shorter, lower-fanout fetch-to-PC path while retaining bubble-free\\\\ndirect calls and jumps.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove the conditional BHT counter array, PC-index lookup, and training ports; retain only validated direct-JAL target prediction with sequential fallback.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Delete decode-stage BHT training wires and instantiate the simplified JAL-only predictor while preserving predicted-next-PC recovery plumbing.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove branch-training outputs and keep existing operand-ready conditional branch comparison and early redirect behavior unchanged.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Retain direct-JAL speculation and verify that sequential conditional fetch plus ID redirect has the required redirect and stall priority.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_branch_predictor.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace counter-state tests with direct-JAL and sequential-conditional lookup tests, including reserved branch encodings.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_pipeline.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add taken and not-taken conditional branch cases, including forwarded and load-dependent operands, to verify decode recovery, RVFI PC order, and direct-JAL fetch behavior.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove the conditional BHT counter array, PC-index lookup, and training ports; retain only validated direct-JAL target prediction with sequential fallback.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Delete decode-stage BHT training wires and instantiate the simplified JAL-only predictor while preserving predicted-next-PC recovery plumbing.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove branch-training outputs and keep existing operand-ready conditional branch comparison and early redirect behavior unchanged.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Retain direct-JAL speculation and verify that sequential conditional fetch plus ID redirect has the required redirect and stall priority.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_branch_predictor.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace counter-state tests with direct-JAL and sequential-conditional lookup tests, including reserved branch encodings.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_pipeline.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add taken and not-taken conditional branch cases, including forwarded and load-dependent operands, to verify decode recovery, RVFI PC order, and direct-JAL fetch behavior.\\\"}], \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"improvement\\\", \\\"fitness\\\": 353.6, \\\"delta_pct\\\": 1.43, \\\"vs_baseline\\\": 25.03, \\\"fmax_mhz\\\": 146.69, \\\"ipc_coremark\\\": 2e-06, \\\"cycles\\\": 4148503, \\\"iterations\\\": 10, \\\"lut4\\\": 8082, \\\"ff\\\": 1900, \\\"seeds\\\": [147.1, 146.69, 145.29], \\\"formal_passed\\\": true, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": true, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"implementation_notes\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-07-11T17:34:38.774222Z\\\", \\\"slot\\\": 2, \\\"round_id\\\": 8, \\\"lesson\\\": \\\"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r8s2 (improvement, +1.43%): After operand-ready conditional branches redirect in ID, removing the BHT's counter lookup, branch-legality decode, target adder, and direction mux from the fetch-to-PC path can outperform its remaining load-dependent-branch benefit while retaining immediate direct-JAL prediction.\\\"}\\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:26:{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260711-001-r9s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Split RVFI retirement-order counter\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The single-issue core increments a live 64-bit RVFI order register at every\\\\nretirement. The FPGA wrapper XOR-reduces RVFI outputs into its LED, so this\\\\ncounter and its wide carry path remain in the placed design even though it\\\\ndoes not affect architectural execution or CoreMark IPC.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The single-issue core increments a live 64-bit RVFI order register at every\\\\nretirement. The FPGA wrapper XOR-reduces RVFI outputs into its LED, so this\\\\ncounter and its wide carry path remain in the placed design even though it\\\\ndoes not affect architectural execution or CoreMark IPC.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the monolithic 64-bit retirement-order incrementer with a low and\\\\nhigh 32-bit counter. Increment the low word on every valid retirement and\\\\nincrement the high word only when the low word wraps; concatenate the words\\\\nfor io_rvfi_order_0. This preserves exact modulo-2^64 strictly increasing\\\\nRVFI order semantics while shortening the common registered carry chain\\\\nfrom 64 to 32 bits, reducing routing pressure without changing IPC.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the 64-bit rvfi_order_q incrementer with carry-coupled 32-bit low/high registers and concatenate them on the RVFI channel-0 order output.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_pipeline.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add retirement-order checks across ordinary valid/bubble cycles and a forced low-word wrap to confirm exact monotonic 64-bit RVFI ordering.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the 64-bit rvfi_order_q incrementer with carry-coupled 32-bit low/high registers and concatenate them on the RVFI channel-0 order output.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_pipeline.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add retirement-order checks across ordinary valid/bubble cycles and a forced low-word wrap to confirm exact monotonic 64-bit RVFI ordering.\\\"}], \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"regression\\\", \\\"fitness\\\": 134.43, \\\"delta_pct\\\": -61.98, \\\"vs_baseline\\\": -52.47, \\\"fmax_mhz\\\": 55.77, \\\"ipc_coremark\\\": 2e-06, \\\"cycles\\\": 4148503, \\\"iterations\\\": 10, \\\"lut4\\\": 7946, \\\"ff\\\": 1888, \\\"seeds\\\": [53.91, 55.77, 58.69], \\\"formal_passed\\\": true, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": true, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"implementation_notes\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-07-11T17:43:57.973602Z\\\", \\\"slot\\\": 0, \\\"round_id\\\": 9, \\\"lesson\\\": \\\"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r9s0 (regression, -61.98%): Splitting a live FPGA-visible 64-bit counter can regress badly when the low-word terminal-count compare and conditional high-word increment add a wide control path that outweighs the shorter common carry chain, so counter decomposition needs post-route evidence rather than carry-width intuition.\\\"}\\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:27:{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260711-002-r9s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Timing-isolated store write buffer\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Random dmem backpressure currently freezes the entire in-order pipe for\\\\nevery store, even though a completed store has no register result and most\\\\nfollowing CoreMark work can execute independently. The failed load bypass\\\\nshows that a same-cycle dmem-to-EX path is too expensive, so stall hiding\\\\nshould be confined to the store-side memory interface instead.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Random dmem backpressure currently freezes the entire in-order pipe for\\\\nevery store, even though a completed store has no register result and most\\\\nfollowing CoreMark work can execute independently. The failed load bypass\\\\nshows that a same-cycle dmem-to-EX path is too expensive, so stall hiding\\\\nshould be confined to the store-side memory interface instead.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a compact one-entry, registered write-behind buffer for stores. An\\\\naccepted store retires normally and enqueues its aligned address, replicated\\\\ndata, and byte mask without waiting for dmemReady; the buffer alone drives\\\\nthe request until accepted. Younger loads and stores stop behind a pending\\\\nstore to preserve ordered memory visibility, while independent ALU and\\\\ncontrol instructions continue. Registering the buffer request and keeping\\\\ndmemReady out of fetch and EX control cones should recover isolated store\\\\nstalls without the placement pressure of a broad front-end queue.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/store_buffer.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a one-entry registered store queue with valid/empty state, stable aligned request fields, and a drain handshake.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Enqueue legal stores into an empty buffer, retain exact RVFI store fields at retirement, and block loads or a second store while an older write is pending.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace store-side global dmem stalls with buffer-full and ordered-memory interlocks while retaining load-response stalls and redirect priority.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Wire the registered buffer to dmem pins and expose its occupancy and enqueue status to the pipeline controls without changing the top-level I/O contract.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_pipeline.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add randomized stalled-store tests covering independent progress, load/store ordering, redirects around pending stores, RVFI order, and exact byte-lane data.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/store_buffer.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a one-entry registered store queue with valid/empty state, stable aligned request fields, and a drain handshake.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Enqueue legal stores into an empty buffer, retain exact RVFI store fields at retirement, and block loads or a second store while an older write is pending.\