{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"jq -r 'select(.category == \\\"micro_opt\\\") | \\\"\\\\(.id) | \\\\(.outcome) | \\\\(.fitness // \\\"-\\\") | \\\\(.delta_pct // \\\"-\\\") | \\\\(.title)\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\njq -r 'select(.id == \\\"hyp-20260711-004-r12s0\\\" or .id == \\\"hyp-20260711-001-r11s0\\\" or .id == \\\"hyp-20260711-001-r3s0\\\" or .id == \\\"hyp-20260711-001-r4s0\\\" or .id == \\\"hyp-20260711-001-r7s0\\\") | {id,title,outcome,fitness,delta_pct,implementation_notes,hypothesis}' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"baseline-bench-7155d3c | improvement | 282.82 | 0.0 | Baseline retest for cores/bench\nhyp-20260711-001-r1s0 | regression | 302.55 | 6.98 | Prune dead EX branch payload\nhyp-20260711-001-r2s0 | broken | - | - | Decode-aware load-use interlock\nhyp-20260711-001-r3s0 | regression | 316.17 | 0.0 | Static backward branch fast path\nhyp-20260711-001-r4s0 | regression | 321.75 | -6.54 | Single narrow RV32M multiplier\nhyp-20260711-001-r5s0 | regression | 262.72 | -23.69 | Authoritative decode branch resolution\nhyp-20260711-001-r6s0 | improvement | 348.63 | 0.95 | Shared magnitude divide datapath\nhyp-20260711-001-r7s0 | broken | - | - | Opcode-aware load-use interlock\nhyp-20260711-001-r8s0 | regression | 188.19 | -46.02 | Unsigned product high-half correction\nhyp-20260711-001-r9s0 | regression | 134.43 | -61.98 | Split RVFI retirement-order counter\nhyp-20260711-001-r10s0 | regression | 147.88 | -58.18 | Register-slice the RVFI retirement bundle\nhyp-20260711-001-r11s0 | regression | 333.04 | -5.81 | Prune duplicate decode writeback bypass\nhyp-20260711-004-r12s0 | regression | 323.42 | -15.03 | Share execute control target adders\nhyp-20260711-004-r13s0 | broken | - | - | Validated load-use source qualification\n{\n  \"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r3s0\",\n  \"title\": \"Static backward branch fast path\",\n  \"outcome\": \"regression\",\n  \"fitness\": 316.17,\n  \"delta_pct\": 0.0,\n  \"implementation_notes\": \"\",\n  \"hypothesis\": {\n    \"motivation\": \"The 16-entry dynamic BHT substantially improved CoreMark by avoiding\\nrecovery bubbles in counted loops, but its indexed counter-array read and\\nupdate state now sit in the fetch-critical logic and reduced placed Fmax.\\nCoreMark's dominant loop-closing conditional branches are direct backward\\nB-type branches whose usual direction is predictable from the immediate.\\n\",\n    \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the PC-indexed two-bit counter lookup with a stateless\\nbackward-taken/forward-not-taken policy for validated conditional branches,\\nwhile retaining direct-JAL target prediction and existing EX comparison\\nrecovery. A negative B-immediate predicts its direct target; forward\\nbranches stay sequential. This removes the BHT flops, index mux, reset\\nfanout, and training cone from fetch, avoids the first-iteration loop miss,\\nand should preserve nearly all CoreMark loop IPC at a higher Fmax.\\n\",\n    \"expected_impact\": {\n      \"fitness_delta_pct\": 5,\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    \"changes\": [\n      {\n        \"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\",\n        \"description\": \"Replace the indexed saturating-counter table and training state with a combinational validated-B-type backward-taken predictor, while keeping direct-JAL prediction.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\",\n        \"description\": \"Remove obsolete execute-to-predictor branch-training connections and retain the predicted-next-PC lookup and recovery wiring.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\",\n        \"description\": \"Remove branch-training outputs that are no longer needed, preserving resolved-next-PC computation and misprediction redirect behavior.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\",\n        \"description\": \"Add directed checks for backward conditional prediction, forward sequential prediction, loop exit recovery, direct JAL prediction, and RVFI PC sequencing.\"\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n}\n{\n  \"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r4s0\",\n  \"title\": \"Single narrow RV32M multiplier\",\n  \"outcome\": \"regression\",\n  \"fitness\": 321.75,\n  \"delta_pct\": -6.54,\n  \"implementation_notes\": \"\",\n  \"hypothesis\": {\n    \"motivation\": \"The ALU currently builds three unconditional 64-by-64 signed-product\\nvariants to serve the four RV32M multiply operations. Those oversized,\\nparallel multiplier cones consume substantial LUT and routing resources on\\nevery cycle, even though RV32 operands need at most a signed 33-by-33\\nproduct to produce every required low or high result bit.\\n\",\n    \"hypothesis\": \"Select operand extension from the decoded multiply operation and compute one\\nsigned 33-by-33 product, selecting its low half for MUL and bits [63:32]\\nfor MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU. This preserves all RV32M signedness semantics\\nwhile eliminating two parallel multipliers and avoiding the accidental\\n64-bit operand expansion. The smaller arithmetic fabric should reduce\\nplacement pressure and improve Fmax without adding latency or changing IPC.\\n\",\n    \"expected_impact\": {\n      \"fitness_delta_pct\": 5,\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    \"changes\": [\n      {\n        \"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\",\n        \"description\": \"Replace the three unconditional 64-bit product expressions with one operation-selected signed 33-by-33 product; retain the exact RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS mappings and all divide/remainder behavior.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\",\n        \"description\": \"Add or retain boundary and randomized vectors for MUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU, including signed-negative and maximal-unsigned operands, to prove the shared product selects the exact RV32M result.\"\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n}\n{\n  \"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r7s0\",\n  \"title\": \"Opcode-aware load-use interlock\",\n  \"outcome\": \"broken\",\n  \"fitness\": null,\n  \"delta_pct\": null,\n  \"implementation_notes\": null,\n  \"hypothesis\": {\n    \"motivation\": \"The current load-use interlock compares a pending load destination with both\\nraw IF/ID register-field bit slices. Several immediate and no-source RV32I\\nforms encode immediate bits in those slices, so a coincidental match inserts\\na bubble even though the following instruction cannot consume the load.\\n\",\n    \"hypothesis\": \"Derive conservative rs1/rs2-use qualifiers locally from the fetched opcode\\nin the hazard unit, and gate each load-use comparison with its qualifier.\\nPreserve both-source interlocks for OP, STORE, and BRANCH, rs1 interlocks\\nfor OP-IMM, LOAD, and JALR, and conservatively treat every other encoding as\\nsource-free. This removes false immediate-field alias bubbles without\\nchanging decoder control, forwarding, memory ordering, or retirement.\\n\",\n    \"expected_impact\": {\n      \"fitness_delta_pct\": 3,\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    \"changes\": [\n      {\n        \"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\",\n        \"description\": \"Decode conservative opcode-local rs1/rs2 use qualifiers and gate load-use comparisons so immediate and source-free encodings cannot create false stalls.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\",\n        \"description\": \"Pass the complete fetched instruction to the hazard unit instead of only its raw rs1 and rs2 bit fields.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\",\n        \"description\": \"Add immediate-alias-after-load cases that must not bubble, alongside OP, STORE, BRANCH, LOAD-address, and JALR dependencies that must still interlock and retire with exact RVFI state.\"\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n}\n{\n  \"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r11s0\",\n  \"title\": \"Prune duplicate decode writeback bypass\",\n  \"outcome\": \"regression\",\n  \"fitness\": 333.04,\n  \"delta_pct\": -5.81,\n  \"implementation_notes\": \"\",\n  \"hypothesis\": {\n    \"motivation\": \"Conditional branches now resolve in ID, so their bypass muxes participate in\\nthe decode-to-PC recovery cone. The register file is explicitly write-first,\\nbut ID repeats a writeback address comparison and data mux for each branch\\nsource before applying the genuinely necessary MEM and EX bypasses.\\n\",\n    \"hypothesis\": \"Remove ID's redundant WB-to-branch operand bypass and its three writeback\\nports. The regfile already returns wb_w_data for a same-cycle matching read\\naddress, making branch_rs1/branch_rs2 identical before the remaining\\nage-prioritized MEM and EX overrides. This eliminates two 5-bit comparators,\\nzero-register tests, and 32-bit mux legs from the early branch-resolution\\ncone, improving Fmax without changing instruction scheduling, IPC, RVFI, or\\nbypass semantics.\\n\",\n    \"expected_impact\": {\n      \"fitness_delta_pct\": 3,\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    \"changes\": [\n      {\n        \"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\",\n        \"description\": \"Delete the redundant writeback bypass inputs and comparison/mux legs from decode-stage branch operand selection, retaining MEM and EX forwarding priority.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\",\n        \"description\": \"Remove the now-unused writeback-to-ID branch-bypass wiring while retaining the register-file write port and all other pipeline connections.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\",\n        \"description\": \"Add a branch whose source register is written in WB during decode, plus EX and MEM override cases, to prove write-first regfile behavior and branch RVFI sequencing remain exact.\"\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n}\n{\n  \"id\": \"hyp-20260711-004-r12s0\",\n  \"title\": \"Share execute control target adders\",\n  \"outcome\": \"regression\",\n  \"fitness\": 323.42,\n  \"delta_pct\": -15.03,\n  \"implementation_notes\": \"\",\n  \"hypothesis\": {\n    \"motivation\": \"The current EX control logic independently builds the same PC-relative\\ntarget for branches and direct JALs, and builds a second rs1-plus-immediate\\nadder for JALR even though its ALU operation is already ADD. These duplicate\\narithmetic cones consume LUTs and routing at the EX/MEM timing endpoint.\\n\",\n    \"hypothesis\": \"Refactor EX control-target generation to compute PC+4 and PC+immediate once\\nper instruction, use the latter for both branch and direct-JAL resolution,\\nand derive the JALR sum from the existing ALU result. This removes duplicate\\n32-bit adders without adding state, changing any redirect timing, or\\nchanging the carried RVFI data, so CoreMark IPC should remain unchanged\\nwhile Tang Nano placement and Fmax improve.\\n\",\n    \"expected_impact\": {\n      \"fitness_delta_pct\": 4,\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    \"changes\": [\n      {\n        \"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\",\n        \"description\": \"Share PC+4 and PC-relative target arithmetic across branch and JAL control paths, and reuse the ADD-class ALU result for the JALR target before bit-zero clearing.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\",\n        \"description\": \"Add directed branch, JAL, JALR, and misaligned-control cases that verify successor PCs, link data, trap behavior, redirect priority, and RVFI retirement order after adder sharing.\"\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
