re carries byte-addressed 32-bit PCs through every pipeline\\nbundle and repeatedly performs PC+4 and PC+immediate arithmetic in fetch,\\ndecode, and execute. RV32I instructions are always four-byte aligned, so\\nthe two low PC bits are invariant zeros on every architecturally executed\\npath. The current JAL-only prediction makes this PC feedback path more\\nimportant to routed Fmax than the removed conditional-BHT logic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current core carries byte-addressed 32-bit PCs through every pipeline\\nbundle and repeatedly performs PC+4 and PC+immediate arithmetic in fetch,\\ndecode, and execute. RV32I instructions are always four-byte aligned, so\\nthe two low PC bits are invariant zeros on every architecturally executed\\npath. The current JAL-only prediction makes this PC feedback path more\\nimportant to routed Fmax than the removed conditional-BHT logic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Rebuild the control-flow spine around a 30-bit word PC internally. Fetch,\\ndirect-JAL prediction, decode-stage branch recovery, and EX branch/JAL\\ntarget generation operate on pc_word plus signed immediate[31:2]; pipeline\\nbundles carry word PCs and word successors. Reconstruct {pc_word,2'b00}\\nonly where an architectural byte address is required (imem, AUIPC/link\\ndata, dmem-derived RVFI context, and RVFI pc fields). JALR retains a\\nfull-width sum solely for its mandated bit-0 clear and bit-1 misalignment\\ntrap. This removes invariant low-bit routing and narrows the pervasive\\nsequential/relative-target adders without changing retirement width,\\nredirect priority, trap rules, or external I/O.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace pipeline PC and successor members with 30-bit word-address representations, retaining helpers or documented reconstruction points for architectural 32-bit PC values.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Store a word-addressed fetch PC, form imemAddr by appending two zero bits, and perform sequential/direct-JAL next-PC selection in word units while retaining redirect-over-stall priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept and return word PCs, derive the signed JAL word displacement from immediate[31:2], and avoid speculating an instruction-address-misaligned JAL target.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Calculate legal operand-ready conditional branch targets and decode redirects in word units, while preserving the existing forwarded compare and misaligned-target trap handoff.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use word-PC arithmetic for AUIPC, branch, JAL, link, and resolved successor paths; retain a full JALR sum for exact alignment semantics and materialize byte PCs only for architectural data.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry the narrower PC payload unchanged through MEM/WB without altering load/store handling or RVFI memory fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Reconstruct full byte PCs and successors at RVFI channel 0, keeping rvfi_order, trap, and all top-level interface fields unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed forward and backward control-flow cases near word-PC wrap, plus aligned and misaligned branch/JAL/JALR checks for RVFI pc_rdata, pc_wdata, trap, and retirement order.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace pipeline PC and successor members with 30-bit word-address representations, retaining helpers or documented reconstruction points for architectural 32-bit PC values.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Store a word-addressed fetch PC, form imemAddr by appending two zero bits, and perform sequential/direct-JAL next-PC selection in word units while retaining redirect-over-stall priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept and return word PCs, derive the signed JAL word displacement from immediate[31:2], and avoid speculating an instruction-address-misaligned JAL target.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Calculate legal operand-ready conditional branch targets and decode redirects in word units, while preserving the existing forwarded compare and misaligned-target trap handoff.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use word-PC arithmetic for AUIPC, branch, JAL, link, and resolved successor paths; retain a full JALR sum for exact alignment semantics and materialize byte PCs only for architectural data.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry the narrower PC payload unchanged through MEM/WB without altering load/store handling or RVFI memory fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Reconstruct full byte PCs and successors at RVFI channel 0, keeping rvfi_order, trap, and all top-level interface fields unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed forward and backward control-flow cases near word-PC wrap, plus aligned and misaligned branch/JAL/JALR checks for RVFI pc_rdata, pc_wdata, trap, and retirement order.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"cosim_failed: coremark.elf\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 10, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r10s1 (broken, n/a): Word-PC control flow must convert JAL/B immediates from byte to word units by dropping both invariant low bits; retaining the encoded halfword bit scales even legal aligned targets incorrectly and causes cosimulation failures.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:33:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r11s1\", \"title\": \"Correct 30-bit word-PC spine\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The fetch PC, predicted successor, and carried retirement PCs are always\\ninstruction aligned, yet the core routes their invariant low two zero bits\\nand uses 32-bit adders in the timing-sensitive fetch and redirect paths.\\nThe prior word-PC attempt failed cosimulation because it retained an\\nencoded halfword immediate bit when converting direct-control offsets, not\\nbecause the reduced-width PC topology is architecturally invalid.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The fetch PC, predicted successor, and carried retirement PCs are always\\ninstruction aligned, yet the core routes their invariant low two zero bits\\nand uses 32-bit adders in the timing-sensitive fetch and redirect paths.\\nThe prior word-PC attempt failed cosimulation because it retained an\\nencoded halfword immediate bit when converting direct-control offsets, not\\nbecause the reduced-width PC topology is architecturally invalid.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Represent sequential fetch, direct-JAL prediction, conditional-branch\\nrecovery, and non-JALR resolved successors as 30-bit instruction-word\\naddresses. PC+4 becomes pc_word+1. Direct JAL must use exactly the signed\\nJ-immediate bits [31:2], and B targets exactly the signed B-immediate bits\\n[31:2], dropping both encoded invariant low bits; this corrects the failed\\nearlier conversion. Reconstruct byte PCs only at imem, AUIPC/link-data,\\nJALR's full-width target/alignment check, and RVFI boundaries. Narrowing\\nthe persistent PC feedback/routing cone should improve Fmax without adding\\nqueueing state or changing single-retirement behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace carried aligned PC and predicted-successor fields with 30-bit word-PC fields while retaining byte-address reconstruction at architectural boundaries.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Store the fetch PC in word units, emit io_imemAddr as {pc_word,2'b00}, and retain redirect-over-stall priority with pc_word+1 sequential advance.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Perform direct-JAL prediction in word units using the exact signed J-immediate [31:2] encoding, with no retained halfword bit.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute decode-resolved conditional branch targets from the exact signed B-immediate [31:2] word displacement while preserving existing operand bypass and trap gating.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use word-PC arithmetic for branch/JAL successors and byte-PC reconstruction for AUIPC/link data; keep JALR full-width until its mandated alignment check completes.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Propagate narrowed PC metadata through MEM/WB and materialize byte-addressed RVFI PC values without altering memory-access semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Adapt predictor, redirect, and RVFI wiring to the internal word-PC representation while preserving the external imem and single-channel RVFI contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add forward/backward JAL and branch cases that verify exact word-offset conversion, plus AUIPC, JALR, misalignment, RVFI PC, and retirement-order coverage.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace carried aligned PC and predicted-successor fields with 30-bit word-PC fields while retaining byte-address reconstruction at architectural boundaries.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Store the fetch PC in word units, emit io_imemAddr as {pc_word,2'b00}, and retain redirect-over-stall priority with pc_word+1 sequential advance.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Perform direct-JAL prediction in word units using the exact signed J-immediate [31:2] encoding, with no retained halfword bit.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute decode-resolved conditional branch targets from the exact signed B-immediate [31:2] word displacement while preserving existing operand bypass and trap gating.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use word-PC arithmetic for branch/JAL successors and byte-PC reconstruction for AUIPC/link data; keep JALR full-width until its mandated alignment check completes.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Propagate narrowed PC metadata through MEM/WB and materialize byte-addressed RVFI PC values without altering memory-access semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Adapt predictor, redirect, and RVFI wiring to the internal word-PC representation while preserving the external imem and single-channel RVFI contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add forward/backward JAL and branch cases that verify exact word-offset conversion, plus AUIPC, JALR, misalignment, RVFI PC, and retirement-order coverage.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 140.48, \"delta_pct\": -60.27, \"vs_baseline\": -50.33, \"fmax_mhz\": 58.28, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4148503, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 7923, \"ff\": 1906, \"seeds\": [58.1, 58.28, 61.98], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T18:13:43.138227Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 11}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:36:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-005-r12s1\", \"title\": \"Registered fetch decode boundary\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current IF/ID payload is combinational, so an instruction-memory read\\ncan feed decoder/control logic and then steer the next PC in the same clock\\ninterval. The earlier two-entry queue reduced stall cycles but its ready,\\nflush, and occupancy logic reduced Fmax more than that IPC benefit repaid.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current IF/ID payload is combinational, so an instruction-memory read\\ncan feed decoder/control logic and then steer the next PC in the same clock\\ninterval. The earlier two-entry queue reduced stall cycles but its ready,\\nflush, and occupancy logic reduced Fmax more than that IPC benefit repaid.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the combinational IF/ID handoff with one registered, valid-tagged\\nfetch buffer that is consumed every cycle by decode. This deliberately\\navoids a queue, prefetch policy, or predictor lookup: when the buffer is\\nconsumed and imem is ready, fetch refills it in the same edge. Decode-stage\\nbranch and direct-JAL resolution remains intact, with redirects invalidating\\nthe younger refill and winning over all fetch enables. Breaking the\\nimem-to-decode-to-PC feedback path should raise Tang Nano Fmax enough to\\noffset the extra recovery slot on control transfers while preserving\\nsingle-retirement RVFI ordering and all bus-stall behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Make the IF/ID payload an explicitly registered fetch-buffer bundle with a valid bit and its sequential-successor metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a one-entry IF/ID register, refill it from imem on a ready cycle, and give older EX and decode redirects priority over stale-buffer capture.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the registered fetch payload while retaining existing decode-stage branch/JAL steering, operand bypasses, and capturable-control gating.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Adjust stall and flush generation for fetch-buffer consume/refill so imem and dmem backpressure cannot duplicate, drop, or replay instructions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire registered IF/ID validity and fetch-buffer control through the pipeline without changing the external imem, dmem, or RVFI interfaces.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed refill, imem-stall, load-use, branch/JAL redirect, and redirect-during-stall tests that check exact RVFI retirement order and PCs.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Make the IF/ID payload an explicitly registered fetch-buffer bundle with a valid bit and its sequential-successor metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a one-entry IF/ID register, refill it from imem on a ready cycle, and give older EX and decode redirects priority over stale-buffer capture.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the registered fetch payload while retaining existing decode-stage branch/JAL steering, operand bypasses, and capturable-control gating.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Adjust stall and flush generation for fetch-buffer consume/refill so imem and dmem backpressure cannot duplicate, drop, or replay instructions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire registered IF/ID validity and fetch-buffer control through the pipeline without changing the external imem, dmem, or RVFI interfaces.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed refill, imem-stall, load-use, branch/JAL redirect, and redirect-during-stall tests that check exact RVFI retirement order and PCs.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 372.83, \"delta_pct\": -2.05, \"vs_baseline\": 31.83, \"fmax_mhz\": 166.36, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4462141, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 7816, \"ff\": 2027, \"seeds\": [157.04, 166.36, 176.09], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T19:09:59.084126Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 12, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-005-r12s1 (regression, -2.05%): A one-entry valid-tagged registered IF/ID buffer can regress CoreMark when its compulsory control-transfer recovery slot and refill/redirect control outweigh the timing relief from cutting the imem-to-decode-to-PC combinational path.\"}\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r1s1 (broken, n/a): An iterative EX divider must preserve the pipeline's valid/retirement progress contract while it holds ID/EX; freezing around a multicycle response can make DIV/REM formal assumptions unreachable and fail liveness even when unrelated instructions pass.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r1s2 (improvement, +11.79%): A compact PC-indexed two-bit BHT that predicts direct B-immediate targets and JALs removes the repeated two-slot EX recovery bubbles in CoreMark's short counted loops, provided EX compares resolved and carried predicted next PCs before flushing.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r2s2 (regression, -2.41%): For this CoreMark front end, a committed four-entry RAS's canonical-JALR decode, stack-top target mux, and EX training state cost more than eliminating return recovery bubbles, so return prediction must stay off the fetch-critical path unless its dynamic benefit is demonstrably larger.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r3s1 (improvement, +8.89%): Resolving conditional branches in ID with EX/MEM/WB operand bypasses and a load-use interlock recovers one CoreMark mispredict slot without extending the fetch predictor's Fmax-critical lookup cone, provided the carried early-resolved bit suppresses EX's duplicate redirect.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r3s2 (regression, -2.47%): On this CoreMark front end, adding a per-entry valid-bit read and BTFNT direction mux to the BHT lookup cone costs more than it saves on first-iteration backward-loop redirects, so cold-start policy must not lengthen the fetch-critical predictor path without a larger benefit.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r5s0 (regression, -23.69%): Once branches are already resolved in ID, carrying a 32-bit successor plus direction and misalignment state through ID/EX only trades EX recomputation for wider registered routing without reducing CoreMark recovery bubbles, so duplicate control logic should remain unless post-route timing proves the payload is cheaper.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r5s2 (improvement, +0.31%): When a legal conditional branch is operand-ready in ID, training its two-bit BHT under the same capture/redirect gate removes a cycle of stale counter state for nearby loop branches and aliases without lengthening the fetch lookup cone.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r6s0 (improvement, +0.95%): Sharing a single unsigned magnitude quotient/remainder cone across DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU modestly reduces divider LUT/routing pressure while preserving RV32M sign, zero-divisor, and INT_MIN/-1 behavior through explicit result restoration.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r6s2 (regression, -1.90%): With a two-bit BHT already in the fetch path, weakly-taken reset harms CoreMark more through cold forward/not-taken branches and aliases than it saves on first counted-loop back edges, so the weakly-not-taken cold bias is the better default.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r7s2 (regression, -8.26%): Decode-resolving canonical JALR returns with bypass readiness, load-dependency, and alignment checks regresses CoreMark when the saved recovery slot on short helper returns cannot amortize the added ID-stage control/target logic, so return recovery needs a lower-cost path or stronger dynamic evidence.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r8s0 (regression, -46.02%): Replacing signed MULH/MULHSU product cones with unsigned-high-half sign corrections can severely hurt the ALU critical path because the multiplier result now feeds conditional 32-bit subtractor chains with sign/operand fanout, so arithmetic sharing must be judged post-route rather than by multiplier-cone count.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r8s1 (regression, -34.19%): A same-cycle dmem-to-EX load bypass can lose badly when its read-data extension and forwarding mux extend the dependent ALU timing cone, so removing a CoreMark load-use bubble requires post-route proof that the memory-to-EX path is faster than the saved cycle.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r8s2 (improvement, +1.43%): After operand-ready conditional branches redirect in ID, removing the BHT's counter lookup, branch-legality decode, target adder, and direction mux from the fetch-to-PC path can outperform its remaining load-dependent-branch benefit while retaining immediate direct-JAL prediction.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r9s0 (regression, -61.98%): Splitting a live FPGA-visible 64-bit counter can regress badly when the low-word terminal-count compare and conditional high-word increment add a wide control path that outweighs the shorter common carry chain, so counter decomposition needs post-route evidence rather than carry-width intuition.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r9s2 (regression, -20.97%): Even a bypass-free `jalr x0, x1, 0` decode redirect regresses CoreMark when x1-pending comparisons, target alignment gating, and the ID early-redirect selection add control/routing cost that common return bubbles cannot amortize, so canonical-return recovery should remain in EX unless its decode path is demonstrably cheaper post-route.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r10s0 (regression, -58.18%): Registering the entire RVFI retirement payload can lose badly on this FPGA because the added wide bundle of flip-flops and routing outweighs any relief from removing MEM/WB and writeback-mux fanout, so high-fanout observability logic needs post-route validation before adding a full-width pipeline stage.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r10s1 (broken, n/a): Word-PC control flow must convert JAL/B immediates from byte to word units by dropping both invariant low bits; retaining the encoded halfword bit scales even legal aligned targets incorrectly and causes cosimulation failures.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r11s2 (improvement, +7.64%): Once ID can capture a legal aligned JAL and carry a resolution marker to suppress EX recovery, moving its immediate target adder out of the fetch-to-PC loop improves CoreMark timing without sacrificing architectural successor or misalignment handling.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-005-r12s1 (regression, -2.05%): A one-entry valid-tagged registered IF/ID buffer can regress CoreMark when its compulsory control-transfer recovery slot and refill/redirect control outweigh the timing relief from cutting the imem-to-decode-to-PC combinational path.\n  - 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-006-r12s2 (regression, -50.95%): Even an invariant PC+4 successor is valuable registered metadata: recomputing `in.pc + 4` in both ID and EX redirect comparisons adds 32-bit arithmetic to recovery control cones, so payload elision must not trade routing for repeated critical-path computation without post-route evidence.\nhyp-20260711-001-r12s0.yaml\nhyp-20260711-002-r12s1.yaml\nhyp-20260711-003-r12s2.yaml\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"jq -r 'select(.category == \\\"structural\\\") | [.id,.title,.outcome,(.fitness // \\\"\\\"),(.delta_pct // \\\"\\\"),(.hypothesis.hypothesis // \\\"\\\")] | @tsv' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"jq -r 'select(.category == \\\"structural\\\") | [.id,.title,.outcome,(.fitness // \\\"\\\"),(.delta_pct // \\\"\\\"),(.hypothesis.hypothesis // \\\"\\\")] | @tsv' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"hyp-20260711-002-r1s1\tIterative divide execution unit\tbroken\t\t\tReplace the combinational divider/remainder datapaths with a dedicated\\n32-cycle restoring divider execution unit. MUL-family operations retain\\nthe FPGA DSP-backed combinational path; a divide request freezes younger\\npipeline state, then injects the exact quotient or remainder into EX/MEM\\nwhen the unit completes. This removes the long divider cone from ordinary\\nEX timing while keeping RV32M corner cases and RVFI retirement intact.\\n\nhyp-20260711-002-r2s1\tBounded radix-4 divide pipeline\tbroken\t\t\tReplace only the real divide/remainder ALU path with an eight-cycle radix-4\\niterative unit. A precise EX interlock will accept each divide exactly once,\\nallow older MEM/WB work to drain, retain the DIV payload while busy, and\\nadvance one normal EX/MEM result on the fixed completion cycle. Eight cycles\\nkeeps total fetch-to-retire latency within the existing 20-step formal depth,\\nwhile removing the large combinational divider from the ordinary EX critical\\npath. MUL remains combinational and the ALTOPS behavior remains equivalent\\nfor formal checks.\\n\nhyp-20260711-002-r3s1\tDecode-stage branch resolution\timprovement\t344.27\t8.89\tResolve conditional branch direction and target in ID using a dedicated\\ndecode bypass network from the preceding EX result, MEM result, and normal\\nwriteback path. An ID-detected mismatch redirects fetch immediately while\\nretaining the branch in ID/EX; a carried early-resolved bit prevents EX from\\nissuing a duplicate redirect. Load-dependent branches retain a precise\\ninterlock until their value is available. This turns direction-mispredict\\nrecovery from two discarded younger slots into one without extending the\\npredictor's fetch-critical lookup cone or changing retirement width.\\n\nhyp-20260711-002-r4s1\tTwo-entry instruction skid queue\tregression\t334.25\t-2.91\tReplace the combinational IF-to-ID payload with a two-entry, PC-tagged\\ninstruction skid queue. Fetch fills an empty queue slot whenever imem is\\nready while ID independently consumes the head, allowing a previously\\nfetched instruction to enter decode during a later imem stall. Each entry\\ncarries its predictor-selected successor so sequential prefetch remains\\ncorrect; EX and ID redirects synchronously invalidate younger queued\\nentries and restart fetch at the resolved target. This keeps the existing\\nsingle-retire pipeline and RVFI boundary intact while converting many\\nisolated zero-wait misses into no-bubble cycles.\\n\nhyp-20260711-002-r5s1\tOne-entry decoupled store buffer\tplacement_failed\t\t\tInsert a one-entry write buffer between MEM and the dmem pins. A store may\\nretire into an empty buffer in one cycle even when dmemReady is low; the\\nbuffer holds its aligned address, replicated data, and byte enables until\\nthe bus accepts it. Loads and a second store wait until the older buffered\\nstore drains, preserving in-order memory visibility, while independent\\nALU/control instructions continue through the existing single-retire\\npipeline. This converts isolated store-side bus stalls into useful work\\nwithout lengthening the predictor lookup or EX arithmetic cone.\\n\nhyp-20260711-002-r6s1\tDecode-stage JALR recovery\tbroken\t\t\tExtend the existing decode-stage control-resolution path to legal JALR\\ninstructions. Reuse its EX/MEM/WB operand bypasses to calculate the masked\\nrs1+immediate target in ID and redirect one cycle before EX whenever the\\nsource is ready and the target is aligned. Carry a single generalized\\ncontrol-resolved bit into EX to suppress duplicate recovery; load-dependent\\nor misaligned JALRs retain the current EX path so their interlock and trap\\nsemantics stay exact. This changes recovery-stage topology without adding a\\ntarget lookup or return stack to the fetch-critical predictor cone.\\n\nhyp-20260711-002-r7s1\tForwarded decode-stage JALR recovery\tbroken\t\t\tResolve legal, aligned JALR targets in ID using the existing EX/MEM/WB\\nbypass priority, then redirect fetch one stage earlier. Crucially, latch\\nthose same selected source operands into ID/EX so EX, register writeback,\\nand RVFI rs1_rdata retain the architecturally current value; the previous\\ndecode-JALR attempt redirected using a forwarded value while retaining a\\nstale operand, which broke the formal register check. Load-dependent and\\nmisaligned JALRs remain on the precise EX path, and a resolved-in-ID flag\\nsuppresses only the duplicate EX redirect.\\n\nhyp-20260711-002-r8s1\tZero-bubble load result bypass\tregression\t229.45\t-34.19\tAdd a MEM-to-EX load-result bypass so the instruction directly following a\\nload enters EX while that load is in MEM, consuming the already\\nsign/zero-extended dmem value instead of its address ALU result. Remove the\\nunconditional load-use interlock; when dmemReady is low, the existing\\ndmem-wide pipeline hold keeps both the load and dependent instruction in\\nplace until the data is valid. This changes the forwarding topology without\\nextending the fetch predictor or changing in-order retirement semantics.\\n\nhyp-20260711-002-r9s1\tTiming-isolated store write buffer\tbroken\t\t\tAdd a compact one-entry, registered write-behind buffer for stores. An\\naccepted store retires normally and enqueues its aligned address, replicated\\ndata, and byte mask without waiting for dmemReady; the buffer alone drives\\nthe request until accepted. Younger loads and stores stop behind a pending\\nstore to preserve ordered memory visibility, while independent ALU and\\ncontrol instructions continue. Registering the buffer request and keeping\\ndmemReady out of fetch and EX control cones should recover isolated store\\nstalls without the placement pressure of a broad front-end queue.\\n\nhyp-20260711-002-r10s1\tWord-addressed control-flow spine\tbroken\t\t\tRebuild the control-flow spine around a 30-bit word PC internally. Fetch,\\ndirect-JAL prediction, decode-stage branch recovery, and EX branch/JAL\\ntarget generation operate on pc_word plus signed immediate[31:2]; pipeline\\nbundles carry word PCs and word successors. Reconstruct {pc_word,2'b00}\\nonly where an architectural byte address is required (imem, AUIPC/link\\ndata, dmem-derived RVFI context, and RVFI pc fields). JALR retains a\\nfull-width sum solely for its mandated bit-0 clear and bit-1 misalignment\\ntrap. This removes invariant low-bit routing and narrows the pervasive\\nsequential/relative-target adders without changing retirement width,\\nredirect priority, trap rules, or external I/O.\\n\nhyp-20260711-002-r11s1\tCorrect 30-bit word-PC spine\tregression\t140.48\t-60.27\tRepresent sequential fetch, direct-JAL prediction, conditional-branch\\nrecovery, and non-JALR resolved successors as 30-bit instruction-word\\naddresses. PC+4 becomes pc_word+1. Direct JAL must use exactly the signed\\nJ-immediate bits [31:2], and B targets exactly the signed B-immediate bits\\n[31:2], dropping both encoded invariant low bits; this corrects the failed\\nearlier conversion. Reconstruct byte PCs only at imem, AUIPC/link-data,\\nJALR's full-width target/alignment check, and RVFI boundaries. Narrowing\\nthe persistent PC feedback/routing cone should improve Fmax without adding\\nqueueing state or changing single-retirement behavior.\\n\nhyp-20260711-005-r12s1\tRegistered fetch decode boundary\tregression\t372.83\t-2.05\tReplace the combinational IF/ID handoff with one registered, valid-tagged\\nfetch buffer that is consumed every cycle by decode. This deliberately\\navoids a queue, prefetch policy, or predictor lookup: when the buffer is\\nconsumed and imem is ready, fetch refills it in the same edge. Decode-stage\\nbranch and direct-JAL resolution remains intact, with redirects invalidating\\nthe younger refill and winning over all fetch enables. Breaking the\\nimem-to-decode-to-PC feedback path should raise Tang Nano Fmax enough to\\noffset the extra recovery slot on control transfers while preserving\\nsingle-retirement RVFI ordering and all bus-stall behavior.\\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
