ete the unused branch_taken and branch_target members from the EX/MEM pipeline bundle.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Stop registering the dead branch payload while retaining branch condition, redirect, and pc_next behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Update the EX/MEM bundle documentation and lint annotations after removing its unused branch payload.\"}, {\n3:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Iterative divide execution unit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The routed critical path runs through the EX combinational arithmetic\\nnetwork, where fully combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU logic is present on\\nevery instruction. CoreMark's divide instructions occur in setup and\\nadaptive timing code, not in its measured iteration loop, so paying this\\nFmax cost continuously is unnecessary.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The routed critical path runs through the EX combinational arithmetic\\nnetwork, where fully combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU logic is present on\\nevery instruction. CoreMark's divide instructions occur in setup and\\nadaptive timing code, not in its measured iteration loop, so paying this\\nFmax cost continuously is unnecessary.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the combinational divider/remainder datapaths with a dedicated\\n32-cycle restoring divider execution unit. MUL-family operations retain\\nthe FPGA DSP-backed combinational path; a divide request freezes younger\\npipeline state, then injects the exact quotient or remainder into EX/MEM\\nwhen the unit completes. This removes the long divider cone from ordinary\\nEX timing while keeping RV32M corner cases and RVFI retirement intact.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove real combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU arithmetic from the normal ALU and retain only the ALTOPS formal \n4:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Compact direct branch predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The five-stage baseline resolves every conditional branch and JAL in EX,\\nflushing two younger instructions whenever control flow changes. CoreMark\\nspends substantial time in short counted loops, so correctly predicting\\ntheir backward branches can recover those repeated front-end bubbles.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The five-stage baseline resolves every conditional branch and JAL in EX,\\nflushing two younger instructions whenever control flow changes. CoreMark\\nspends substantial time in short counted loops, so correctly predicting\\ntheir backward branches can recover those repeated front-end bubbles.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small PC-indexed two-bit direction table in the fetch path. For a\\nvalidated conditional branch, use its fetched B-immediate as the direct\\ntarget when the counter predicts taken; predict direct JALs taken as well.\\nCarry the predicted next PC to EX, update the counter on branch resolution,\\nand redirect/flush only when resolved control flow differs from the\\nprediction. JALR remains predict-not-taken, keeping the predictor compact\\nand avoiding a costly indirect-target structure near the Fmax limit.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a compact PC-indexed two-bit counter predictor that recognizes validated B-type branches and\n5:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r2s0\", \"title\": \"Decode-aware load-use interlock\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The load-use detector presently compares a pending load destination against\\nboth instruction bit fields [19:15] and [24:20] unconditionally. Many\\nRV32I encodings use one or both fields as immediate bits rather than source\\nregisters, so an accidental bit match inserts a needless pipeline bubble.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The load-use detector presently compares a pending load destination against\\nboth instruction bit fields [19:15] and [24:20] unconditionally. Many\\nRV32I encodings use one or both fields as immediate bits rather than source\\nregisters, so an accidental bit match inserts a needless pipeline bubble.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Carry explicit rs1/rs2-use qualification from the validated decoder to the\\nload-use interlock. Stall only when the following instruction truly reads\\nthe matching source register; retain stalls for real ALU, branch, store,\\nJALR, and address dependencies. This removes false post-load bubbles in\\nCoreMark's immediate-heavy code without changing pipeline depth, forwarding,\\nmemory timing, or retirement semantics.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose validated uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 decode signals, with both false by default and set only for instruction forms that architecturally consume each operand.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.s\n6:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Bounded radix-4 divide pipeline\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The fully combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU cone is paid in every EX-stage\\ntiming analysis even though CoreMark's measured inner loops rarely execute\\ndivision. The earlier 32-cycle replacement confirmed the opportunity but\\nexceeded the bounded formal-retirement window and broke liveness.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The fully combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU cone is paid in every EX-stage\\ntiming analysis even though CoreMark's measured inner loops rarely execute\\ndivision. The earlier 32-cycle replacement confirmed the opportunity but\\nexceeded the bounded formal-retirement window and broke liveness.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace only the real divide/remainder ALU path with an eight-cycle radix-4\\niterative unit. A precise EX interlock will accept each divide exactly once,\\nallow older MEM/WB work to drain, retain the DIV payload while busy, and\\nadvance one normal EX/MEM result on the fixed completion cycle. Eight cycles\\nkeeps total fetch-to-retire latency within the existing 20-step formal depth,\\nwhile removing the large combinational divider from the ordinary EX critical\\npath. MUL remains combinational and the ALTOPS behavior remains equivalent\\nfor formal checks.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 7, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove real combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU selection from the no\n7:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-003-r2s2\", \"title\": \"Committed return-stack JALR predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current BHT eliminates the repeated recovery bubbles for direct branches\\nand JALs, but every function return remains an unpredicted JALR. CoreMark's\\nsmall routines execute many call and return pairs, making return recovery a\\nconcentrated remaining front-end cost.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current BHT eliminates the repeated recovery bubbles for direct branches\\nand JALs, but every function return remains an unpredicted JALR. CoreMark's\\nsmall routines execute many call and return pairs, making return recovery a\\nconcentrated remaining front-end cost.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Extend the existing direct predictor with a tiny committed return-address\\nstack. Detect canonical RV32 calls and returns in fetch: predict a JALR\\nreturn through the stack top, while EX pushes PC+4 for resolved calls and\\npops only resolved canonical returns. Keeping updates at resolution avoids\\ncorrupting predictor state on a mispredicted speculative path, and avoids\\nthe tags, target RAM, and lookup delay of a general indirect BTB.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a shallow committed return-address stack, canonical JAL/JALR call and return recognition, resolved-call push/pop training, and stack-top prediction for returns while retaining the BHT behavior.\"}, {\"f\n8:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r3s0\", \"title\": \"Static backward branch fast path\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The 16-entry dynamic BHT substantially improved CoreMark by avoiding\\nrecovery bubbles in counted loops, but its indexed counter-array read and\\nupdate state now sit in the fetch-critical logic and reduced placed Fmax.\\nCoreMark's dominant loop-closing conditional branches are direct backward\\nB-type branches whose usual direction is predictable from the immediate.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The 16-entry dynamic BHT substantially improved CoreMark by avoiding\\nrecovery bubbles in counted loops, but its indexed counter-array read and\\nupdate state now sit in the fetch-critical logic and reduced placed Fmax.\\nCoreMark's dominant loop-closing conditional branches are direct backward\\nB-type branches whose usual direction is predictable from the immediate.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the PC-indexed two-bit counter lookup with a stateless\\nbackward-taken/forward-not-taken policy for validated conditional branches,\\nwhile retaining direct-JAL target prediction and existing EX comparison\\nrecovery. A negative B-immediate predicts its direct target; forward\\nbranches stay sequential. This removes the BHT flops, index mux, reset\\nfanout, and training cone from fetch, avoids the first-iteration loop miss,\\nand should preserve nearly all CoreMark loop IPC at a higher Fmax.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\"\n9:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Decode-stage branch resolution\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The compact BHT has removed the repeated recovery cost of correctly\\npredicted loop branches, but direction changes and predictor alias/cold\\nmisses still redirect only after the instruction reaches EX. Each of those\\nevents currently discards two younger fetch/decode slots. The prior return\\npredictor showed that adding more lookup logic to fetch harms Fmax, so the\\nremaining recovery latency should be reduced behind the fetch boundary.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The compact BHT has removed the repeated recovery cost of correctly\\npredicted loop branches, but direction changes and predictor alias/cold\\nmisses still redirect only after the instruction reaches EX. Each of those\\nevents currently discards two younger fetch/decode slots. The prior return\\npredictor showed that adding more lookup logic to fetch harms Fmax, so the\\nremaining recovery latency should be reduced behind the fetch boundary.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Resolve conditional branch direction and target in ID using a dedicated\\ndecode bypass network from the preceding EX result, MEM result, and normal\\nwriteback path. An ID-detected mismatch redirects fetch immediately while\\nretaining the branch in ID/EX; a carried early-resolved bit prevents EX from\\nissuing a duplicate redirect. Load-dependent branches retain a precise\\ninterlock until their value is available. This turns direction-mispredi\n10:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-003-r3s2\", \"title\": \"Cold-start BTFNT branch fallback\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current two-bit BHT is initialized weakly not taken, so every cold\\nbackward loop branch incurs an avoidable EX recovery before it trains.\\nCoreMark repeatedly enters short counted loops, making this warm-up cost\\nvisible even after the main BHT improvement.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current two-bit BHT is initialized weakly not taken, so every cold\\nbackward loop branch incurs an avoidable EX recovery before it trains.\\nCoreMark repeatedly enters short counted loops, making this warm-up cost\\nvisible even after the main BHT improvement.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add one validity bit per existing BHT entry and use static backward-taken,\\nforward-not-taken prediction only while that entry is untrained. On the\\nfirst resolved branch, mark the entry valid and retain the present two-bit\\nsaturating-counter behavior. This removes cold backward-loop redirects\\nwithout enlarging the target structure or putting a return-stack lookup on\\nthe fetch critical path; trained branches retain dynamic behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add per-entry valid state, initialize entries invalid, select BTFNT from the B-immediate sign for untrained legal branches, and set validity on resolved branch training.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"A\n12:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r4s1\", \"title\": \"Two-entry instruction skid queue\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The CoreMark cosim injects roughly 22% instruction-bus backpressure, and\\nthe current front end turns every unready imem cycle into a full fetch and\\ndecode bubble. The successful branch work has already reduced redirect\\nwaste, so absorbing independent delivery stalls is now a larger structural\\nsource of lost issue slots.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The CoreMark cosim injects roughly 22% instruction-bus backpressure, and\\nthe current front end turns every unready imem cycle into a full fetch and\\ndecode bubble. The successful branch work has already reduced redirect\\nwaste, so absorbing independent delivery stalls is now a larger structural\\nsource of lost issue slots.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the combinational IF-to-ID payload with a two-entry, PC-tagged\\ninstruction skid queue. Fetch fills an empty queue slot whenever imem is\\nready while ID independently consumes the head, allowing a previously\\nfetched instruction to enter decode during a later imem stall. Each entry\\ncarries its predictor-selected successor so sequential prefetch remains\\ncorrect; EX and ID redirects synchronously invalidate younger queued\\nentries and restart fetch at the resolved target. This keeps the existing\\nsingle-retire pipeline and RVFI boundary intact while converting many\\nisolated zero-wait misses into no-bubble cycles.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\":\n13:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-003-r4s2\", \"title\": \"Folded-PC BHT indexing\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The profitable 16-entry BHT currently indexes only PC[5:2], so branch\\nsites separated by 64-byte code regions can repeatedly overwrite the\\nsame two-bit counter. CoreMark has several small hot routines and loops,\\nmaking destructive direction aliasing a likely remaining source of\\nrecoveries after decode-stage branch resolution.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The profitable 16-entry BHT currently indexes only PC[5:2], so branch\\nsites separated by 64-byte code regions can repeatedly overwrite the\\nsame two-bit counter. CoreMark has several small hot routines and loops,\\nmaking destructive direction aliasing a likely remaining source of\\nrecoveries after decode-stage branch resolution.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the direct BHT index with a four-bit folded-PC hash, for example\\nPC[5:2] XOR PC[9:6], for both lookup and resolved-branch training. This\\nretains the existing 16 counters, reset policy, direct B-immediate target\\npath, and JAL behavior, but distributes otherwise congruent branch PCs\\nacross the table. Four local XOR gates should be materially cheaper than\\nanother predictor state array or target lookup, while fewer alias-driven\\ndirection misses improve CoreMark IPC.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Derive the shared lookup and training BHT indices from \n14:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r5s0\", \"title\": \"Authoritative decode branch resolution\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current decode stage already compares fully bypassed branch operands and\\ncomputes the direct successor, yet EX repeats the branch comparison and\\ntarget calculation before registering the same RVFI pc_wdata result. This\\nduplicate conditional-control cone adds LUTs and routing to the EX/MEM\\nendpoint after the successful early-resolution optimization.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current decode stage already compares fully bypassed branch operands and\\ncomputes the direct successor, yet EX repeats the branch comparison and\\ntarget calculation before registering the same RVFI pc_wdata result. This\\nduplicate conditional-control cone adds LUTs and routing to the EX/MEM\\nendpoint after the successful early-resolution optimization.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Make the decode-stage branch decision authoritative for every legal\\nconditional branch once its operands are ready. Carry the resolved taken\\nbit, successor, and target-misalignment fault state through ID/EX so EX\\nselects the recorded result, trains the BHT from the recorded direction,\\nand preserves precise illegal/trap behavior without recomputing a branch\\ncomparator or PC-plus-immediate adder. This should shorten the EX control\\npath while retaining the existing load interlock and recovery semantics.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\n15:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"One-entry decoupled store buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Random dmem backpressure currently freezes every pipeline stage whenever a\\nstore reaches EX/MEM, even though a completed store has no register result\\nand most following CoreMark instructions are independent. The failed fetch\\nqueue showed that broad front-end buffering costs too much Fmax, so the\\nnext decoupling point should be off the critical fetch and execute paths.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Random dmem backpressure currently freezes every pipeline stage whenever a\\nstore reaches EX/MEM, even though a completed store has no register result\\nand most following CoreMark instructions are independent. The failed fetch\\nqueue showed that broad front-end buffering costs too much Fmax, so the\\nnext decoupling point should be off the critical fetch and execute paths.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Insert a one-entry write buffer between MEM and the dmem pins. A store may\\nretire into an empty buffer in one cycle even when dmemReady is low; the\\nbuffer holds its aligned address, replicated data, and byte enables until\\nthe bus accepts it. Loads and a second store wait until the older buffered\\nstore drains, preserving in-order memory visibility, while independent\\nALU/control instructions continue through the existing single-retire\\npipeline. This converts isolated store-side bus stalls into useful work\\nwithout lengthening the predictor lookup or EX arithmetic co\n16:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-003-r5s2\", \"title\": \"Decode-stage BHT training\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Conditional branches are now resolved with current operands in ID, but the\\nBHT is still updated one stage later in EX. That leaves the predictor using\\nstale counter state for the next fetches after a direction change, despite\\nthe correct branch outcome already being available without another ALU.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Conditional branches are now resolved with current operands in ID, but the\\nBHT is still updated one stage later in EX. That leaves the predictor using\\nstale counter state for the next fetches after a direction change, despite\\nthe correct branch outcome already being available without another ALU.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Train the existing two-bit BHT directly from the validated, operand-ready\\ndecode-stage branch result and remove the redundant EX-to-predictor training\\npath. This advances counter updates by one cycle without changing BHT size,\\nindexing, reset policy, direct-target generation, or the fetch lookup cone.\\nIt should reduce short-loop and closely spaced alias recovery bubbles while\\npreserving the successful ID-stage redirect and EX fallback behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose a valid, non-stalled decode-stage branch-training event carrying the resolved branch PC and direction, gated until operands are forwardable an\n17:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r6s0\", \"title\": \"Shared magnitude divide datapath\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The ALU currently describes separate signed and unsigned quotient and\\nremainder operators. Those four large combinational arithmetic cones are\\nselectable on every EX cycle, creating avoidable LUT and routing pressure\\neven though every RV32M divide operation needs only one result form.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The ALU currently describes separate signed and unsigned quotient and\\nremainder operators. Those four large combinational arithmetic cones are\\nselectable on every EX cycle, creating avoidable LUT and routing pressure\\neven though every RV32M divide operation needs only one result form.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the four DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU expressions with a shared magnitude\\nquotient/remainder datapath: derive operand magnitudes only for signed\\noperations, perform one unsigned divide and modulo pair, and restore the\\nquotient or remainder sign according to RV32M rules. Keep explicit\\ndivide-by-zero behavior and retain the magnitude-based INT_MIN/-1 results.\\nThis halves the divider-class operator count without changing latency,\\nforwarding, retirement, or the fetch-critical predictor path, so it should\\nimprove Tang Nano placement and Fmax while preserving CoreMark IPC.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace four signed/unsigned DIV and REM expres\n18:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r6s1\", \"title\": \"Decode-stage JALR recovery\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Conditional branches already recover in decode, but every JALR still\\nreaches EX before the core discovers that sequential fetch is wrong. CoreMark\\nuses call and return control flow often enough that this leaves a repeated\\ntwo-slot recovery penalty after the BHT has removed most loop-branch waste.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Conditional branches already recover in decode, but every JALR still\\nreaches EX before the core discovers that sequential fetch is wrong. CoreMark\\nuses call and return control flow often enough that this leaves a repeated\\ntwo-slot recovery penalty after the BHT has removed most loop-branch waste.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Extend the existing decode-stage control-resolution path to legal JALR\\ninstructions. Reuse its EX/MEM/WB operand bypasses to calculate the masked\\nrs1+immediate target in ID and redirect one cycle before EX whenever the\\nsource is ready and the target is aligned. Carry a single generalized\\ncontrol-resolved bit into EX to suppress duplicate recovery; load-dependent\\nor misaligned JALRs retain the current EX path so their interlock and trap\\nsemantics stay exact. This changes recovery-stage topology without adding a\\ntarget lookup or return stack to the fetch-critical predictor cone.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Generalize\n20:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r7s0\", \"title\": \"Opcode-aware load-use interlock\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current load-use interlock compares a pending load destination with both\\nraw IF/ID register-field bit slices. Several immediate and no-source RV32I\\nforms encode immediate bits in those slices, so a coincidental match inserts\\na bubble even though the following instruction cannot consume the load.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current load-use interlock compares a pending load destination with both\\nraw IF/ID register-field bit slices. Several immediate and no-source RV32I\\nforms encode immediate bits in those slices, so a coincidental match inserts\\na bubble even though the following instruction cannot consume the load.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Derive conservative rs1/rs2-use qualifiers locally from the fetched opcode\\nin the hazard unit, and gate each load-use comparison with its qualifier.\\nPreserve both-source interlocks for OP, STORE, and BRANCH, rs1 interlocks\\nfor OP-IMM, LOAD, and JALR, and conservatively treat every other encoding as\\nsource-free. This removes false immediate-field alias bubbles without\\nchanging decoder control, forwarding, memory ordering, or retirement.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Decode conservative opcode-local rs1/rs2 use qualifiers and gate load-use comparisons so immediate and source-free encodings cannot create fals\n21:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r7s1\", \"title\": \"Forwarded decode-stage JALR recovery\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current predictor already removes direct-JAL and steady-state branch\\nbubbles, but every JALR still waits for EX to repair the sequential fetch\\npath. CoreMark's calls and returns make that recurring recovery cost a\\nmeaningful remaining front-end loss.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current predictor already removes direct-JAL and steady-state branch\\nbubbles, but every JALR still waits for EX to repair the sequential fetch\\npath. CoreMark's calls and returns make that recurring recovery cost a\\nmeaningful remaining front-end loss.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Resolve legal, aligned JALR targets in ID using the existing EX/MEM/WB\\nbypass priority, then redirect fetch one stage earlier. Crucially, latch\\nthose same selected source operands into ID/EX so EX, register writeback,\\nand RVFI rs1_rdata retain the architecturally current value; the previous\\ndecode-JALR attempt redirected using a forwarded value while retaining a\\nstale operand, which broke the formal register check. Load-dependent and\\nmisaligned JALRs remain on the precise EX path, and a resolved-in-ID flag\\nsuppresses only the duplicate EX redirect.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Generalize the ID/EX branch-resolved marker into a control-resolved-in-ID marker while retaining the existing compa\n22:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-003-r7s2\", \"title\": \"Decode-assisted return recovery\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Direct branches and JALs already avoid most front-end recovery, but canonical\\nfunction returns still default to the sequential path until EX. CoreMark's\\nsmall helper routines make this a recurring remaining control-flow penalty.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Direct branches and JALs already avoid most front-end recovery, but canonical\\nfunction returns still default to the sequential path until EX. CoreMark's\\nsmall helper routines make this a recurring remaining control-flow penalty.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Recognize only canonical returns (JALR x0, x1, 0) in decode and use the\\nexisting EX/MEM/WB bypass network to form the aligned x1 target there. When\\nx1 is ready, redirect fetch one cycle earlier and mark the control transfer\\nresolved so EX preserves its normal RVFI pc_wdata without issuing a duplicate\\nredirect. This is a return-specific late predictor: unlike the prior RAS, it\\nadds no state, tag, or target mux to the fetch-critical BHT lookup cone; unlike\\nthe broad JALR experiment, it leaves calls and arbitrary indirect jumps on the\\nproven EX path. Load-dependent or misaligned returns retain EX resolution and\\nexact trap behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Generalize the ID/EX branch-resolved marker to denote a control transfer resolved in decode,\n23:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r8s0\", \"title\": \"Unsigned product high-half correction\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current ALU materializes three unconditional multiplier variants even\\nthough all four RV32M multiply operations share the same unsigned 32 by 32\\npartial product. The prior signed 33-bit shared multiplier regressed, so a\\nsmaller correction-based reuse of the already efficient unsigned product is\\nthe safer remaining arithmetic optimization.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current ALU materializes three unconditional multiplier variants even\\nthough all four RV32M multiply operations share the same unsigned 32 by 32\\npartial product. The prior signed 33-bit shared multiplier regressed, so a\\nsmaller correction-based reuse of the already efficient unsigned product is\\nthe safer remaining arithmetic optimization.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep one unsigned 32 by 32 product for MUL and MULHU, then derive MULH and\\nMULHSU high halves from that product with modulo-2^32 sign corrections:\\nsubtract b when rs1 is negative and subtract a when rs2 is negative, with\\nonly the applicable term used for MULHSU. This removes the two wide signed\\nproduct cones without changing EX latency, forwarding, retirement, or the\\nproven divide datapath. It should reduce LUT/routing pressure and raise Fmax\\nwhile preserving CoreMark IPC.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace mul_ss a\n24:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r8s1\", \"title\": \"Zero-bubble load result bypass\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage pipeline always inserts a load-use bubble even\\nthough the benchmark memory interface returns read data combinationally\\nwhenever dmemReady is high. CoreMark has frequent load-fed arithmetic and\\npointer updates, so this compulsory bubble directly lowers iterations per\\ncycle under the zero-wait and randomly stalled evaluation model.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage pipeline always inserts a load-use bubble even\\nthough the benchmark memory interface returns read data combinationally\\nwhenever dmemReady is high. CoreMark has frequent load-fed arithmetic and\\npointer updates, so this compulsory bubble directly lowers iterations per\\ncycle under the zero-wait and randomly stalled evaluation model.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a MEM-to-EX load-result bypass so the instruction directly following a\\nload enters EX while that load is in MEM, consuming the already\\nsign/zero-extended dmem value instead of its address ALU result. Remove the\\nunconditional load-use interlock; when dmemReady is low, the existing\\ndmem-wide pipeline hold keeps both the load and dependent instruction in\\nplace until the data is valid. This changes the forwarding topology without\\nextending the fetch predictor or changing in-order retirement semantics.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rt\n25:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-003-r8s2\", \"title\": \"JAL-only fetch predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Conditional branches now resolve in ID with bypassing, so a sequential\\nconditional fetch can usually be repaired at the same edge without an\\nextra decode bubble. The BHT still places a counter-array mux, branch\\nlegality decode, immediate-target adder, and direction mux on every\\ninstruction-fetch path. Direct JALs cannot use the ID branch mechanism\\nand remain worth predicting at fetch.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Conditional branches now resolve in ID with bypassing, so a sequential\\nconditional fetch can usually be repaired at the same edge without an\\nextra decode bubble. The BHT still places a counter-array mux, branch\\nlegality decode, immediate-target adder, and direction mux on every\\ninstruction-fetch path. Direct JALs cannot use the ID branch mechanism\\nand remain worth predicting at fetch.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the conditional two-bit BHT prediction with a minimal direct-JAL\\nfetch predictor: JAL is still sent immediately to its encoded target, but\\nconditional branches always fetch PC+4 and rely on the established\\noperand-ready ID redirect for taken outcomes. Remove BHT state and its\\ndecode-stage training interface. This trades the limited remaining benefit\\nof speculative conditional direction (principally load-dependent branches)\\nfor a shorter, lower-fanout fetch-to-PC path while retaining bubble-free\\ndirect calls and jumps.\\n\", \"expec\n26:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r9s0\", \"title\": \"Split RVFI retirement-order counter\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The single-issue core increments a live 64-bit RVFI order register at every\\nretirement. The FPGA wrapper XOR-reduces RVFI outputs into its LED, so this\\ncounter and its wide carry path remain in the placed design even though it\\ndoes not affect architectural execution or CoreMark IPC.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The single-issue core increments a live 64-bit RVFI order register at every\\nretirement. The FPGA wrapper XOR-reduces RVFI outputs into its LED, so this\\ncounter and its wide carry path remain in the placed design even though it\\ndoes not affect architectural execution or CoreMark IPC.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the monolithic 64-bit retirement-order incrementer with a low and\\nhigh 32-bit counter. Increment the low word on every valid retirement and\\nincrement the high word only when the low word wraps; concatenate the words\\nfor io_rvfi_order_0. This preserves exact modulo-2^64 strictly increasing\\nRVFI order semantics while shortening the common registered carry chain\\nfrom 64 to 32 bits, reducing routing pressure without changing IPC.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the 64-bit rvfi_order_q incrementer with carry-coupled 32-bit low/high registers and concatenate them on the RVFI channel-0 order output.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\",\n27:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r9s1\", \"title\": \"Timing-isolated store write buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Random dmem backpressure currently freezes the entire in-order pipe for\\nevery store, even though a completed store has no register result and most\\nfollowing CoreMark work can execute independently. The failed load bypass\\nshows that a same-cycle dmem-to-EX path is too expensive, so stall hiding\\nshould be confined to the store-side memory interface instead.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Random dmem backpressure currently freezes the entire in-order pipe for\\nevery store, even though a completed store has no register result and most\\nfollowing CoreMark work can execute independently. The failed load bypass\\nshows that a same-cycle dmem-to-EX path is too expensive, so stall hiding\\nshould be confined to the store-side memory interface instead.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a compact one-entry, registered write-behind buffer for stores. An\\naccepted store retires normally and enqueues its aligned address, replicated\\ndata, and byte mask without waiting for dmemReady; the buffer alone drives\\nthe request until accepted. Younger loads and stores stop behind a pending\\nstore to preserve ordered memory visibility, while independent ALU and\\ncontrol instructions continue. Registering the buffer request and keeping\\ndmemReady out of fetch and EX control cones should recover isolated store\\nstalls without the placement pressure of a broad front-end queue.\\n\", \"expec\n28:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-003-r9s2\", \"title\": \"Clean-register return fast predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The JAL-only fetch path has restored Fmax, while conditional branches already\\nrecover in decode. Canonical returns remain a recurring two-slot EX recovery,\\nbut the previous general decode-return path lost heavily to its bypass and\\ntarget-calculation logic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The JAL-only fetch path has restored Fmax, while conditional branches already\\nrecover in decode. Canonical returns remain a recurring two-slot EX recovery,\\nbut the previous general decode-return path lost heavily to its bypass and\\ntarget-calculation logic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a conservative decode-stage fast path only for the exact canonical return\\nencoding `jalr x0, x1, 0` when no older EX or MEM writer targets x1. Its target\\nis the clean regfile x1 value with bit zero cleared, so this path needs neither\\noperand-bypass muxes nor a 32-bit add. If x1 is pending, the target is\\nmisaligned, or the pipeline is stalled, retain normal EX-stage JALR recovery\\nand trap behavior. This can recover common leaf/helper returns without adding\\nstate or lengthening the fetch predictor cone.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Rename or generalize the carried decode-resolution bit so EX can suppress only a verified decode-resolved canonical return.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"d\n29:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r10s0\", \"title\": \"Register-slice the RVFI retirement bundle\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The FPGA benchmark deliberately XOR-reduces every RVFI output into the LED,\\nso the complete retirement payload remains a live, high-fanout timing cone.\\nToday fields from MEM/WB and the combinational writeback mux drive that\\nexternal reduction directly, even though RVFI consumers only require an\\nordered stream of architectural retirements rather than zero extra latency.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The FPGA benchmark deliberately XOR-reduces every RVFI output into the LED,\\nso the complete retirement payload remains a live, high-fanout timing cone.\\nToday fields from MEM/WB and the combinational writeback mux drive that\\nexternal reduction directly, even though RVFI consumers only require an\\nordered stream of architectural retirements rather than zero extra latency.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add one dedicated RVFI output register in core.sv. Capture every channel-0\\nRVFI field and the current order value atomically when MEM/WB retires, then\\ndrive the top-level ports solely from that register. The extra fixed\\nobservation cycle leaves execution, marker timing, and retirement order\\nunchanged while cutting the MEM/WB/writeback-to-wrapper fanout path. This\\nshould improve placed Fmax without extending fetch, ALU, memory, or branch\\ntiming cones.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.\n30:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r10s1\", \"title\": \"Word-addressed control-flow spine\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current core carries byte-addressed 32-bit PCs through every pipeline\\nbundle and repeatedly performs PC+4 and PC+immediate arithmetic in fetch,\\ndecode, and execute. RV32I instructions are always four-byte aligned, so\\nthe two low PC bits are invariant zeros on every architecturally executed\\npath. The current JAL-only prediction makes this PC feedback path more\\nimportant to routed Fmax than the removed conditional-BHT logic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current core carries byte-addressed 32-bit PCs through every pipeline\\nbundle and repeatedly performs PC+4 and PC+immediate arithmetic in fetch,\\ndecode, and execute. RV32I instructions are always four-byte aligned, so\\nthe two low PC bits are invariant zeros on every architecturally executed\\npath. The current JAL-only prediction makes this PC feedback path more\\nimportant to routed Fmax than the removed conditional-BHT logic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Rebuild the control-flow spine around a 30-bit word PC internally. Fetch,\\ndirect-JAL prediction, decode-stage branch recovery, and EX branch/JAL\\ntarget generation operate on pc_word plus signed immediate[31:2]; pipeline\\nbundles carry word PCs and word successors. Reconstruct {pc_word,2'b00}\\nonly where an architectural byte address is required (imem, AUIPC/link\\ndata, dmem-derived RVFI context, and RVFI pc fields). JALR retains a\\nfull-width sum\n31:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-003-r10s2\", \"title\": \"Decode-resolved direct JAL path\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current JAL-only predictor improved fitness by keeping direct JALs off\\nthe EX recovery path, but it still extracts a J-immediate and performs a\\nPC-relative target add on every fetch-to-PC path. Conditional branches\\nalready demonstrate that an operand-ready decode redirect can recover\\ncontrol flow without a decode bubble.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current JAL-only predictor improved fitness by keeping direct JALs off\\nthe EX recovery path, but it still extracts a J-immediate and performs a\\nPC-relative target add on every fetch-to-PC path. Conditional branches\\nalready demonstrate that an operand-ready decode redirect can recover\\ncontrol flow without a decode bubble.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace fetch-stage direct-JAL prediction with a decode-stage direct-JAL\\nredirect that reuses the existing ID redirect priority and target path.\\nJAL has no source dependency, so its legal aligned target can steer the PC\\non the edge that captures the JAL into ID/EX, preserving the current\\nno-bubble control-flow behavior. Carry a decode-resolved control marker to\\nsuppress EX's duplicate JAL recovery; leave misaligned JAL targets on the\\nexisting EX trap path. This removes J-immediate extraction, target addition,\\nand the predictor mux from the fetch-critical path while avoiding the bypass,\\npending-register, and indirect-target logic that made return fas\n32:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r11s0\", \"title\": \"Prune duplicate decode writeback bypass\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"Conditional branches now resolve in ID, so their bypass muxes participate in\\nthe decode-to-PC recovery cone. The register file is explicitly write-first,\\nbut ID repeats a writeback address comparison and data mux for each branch\\nsource before applying the genuinely necessary MEM and EX bypasses.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Conditional branches now resolve in ID, so their bypass muxes participate in\\nthe decode-to-PC recovery cone. The register file is explicitly write-first,\\nbut ID repeats a writeback address comparison and data mux for each branch\\nsource before applying the genuinely necessary MEM and EX bypasses.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Remove ID's redundant WB-to-branch operand bypass and its three writeback\\nports. The regfile already returns wb_w_data for a same-cycle matching read\\naddress, making branch_rs1/branch_rs2 identical before the remaining\\nage-prioritized MEM and EX overrides. This eliminates two 5-bit comparators,\\nzero-register tests, and 32-bit mux legs from the early branch-resolution\\ncone, improving Fmax without changing instruction scheduling, IPC, RVFI, or\\nbypass semantics.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Delete the redundant writeback bypass inputs and comparison/mux legs from decode-stage branch operand selection, retaini\n33:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r11s1\", \"title\": \"Correct 30-bit word-PC spine\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The fetch PC, predicted successor, and carried retirement PCs are always\\ninstruction aligned, yet the core routes their invariant low two zero bits\\nand uses 32-bit adders in the timing-sensitive fetch and redirect paths.\\nThe prior word-PC attempt failed cosimulation because it retained an\\nencoded halfword immediate bit when converting direct-control offsets, not\\nbecause the reduced-width PC topology is architecturally invalid.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The fetch PC, predicted successor, and carried retirement PCs are always\\ninstruction aligned, yet the core routes their invariant low two zero bits\\nand uses 32-bit adders in the timing-sensitive fetch and redirect paths.\\nThe prior word-PC attempt failed cosimulation because it retained an\\nencoded halfword immediate bit when converting direct-control offsets, not\\nbecause the reduced-width PC topology is architecturally invalid.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Represent sequential fetch, direct-JAL prediction, conditional-branch\\nrecovery, and non-JALR resolved successors as 30-bit instruction-word\\naddresses. PC+4 becomes pc_word+1. Direct JAL must use exactly the signed\\nJ-immediate bits [31:2], and B targets exactly the signed B-immediate bits\\n[31:2], dropping both encoded invariant low bits; this corrects the failed\\nearlier conversion. Reconstruct byte PCs only at imem, AUIPC/link-data,\\nJALR's full-wid\n34:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-003-r11s2\", \"title\": \"Aligned decode-stage JAL steering\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current JAL-only predictor avoids direct-JAL recovery bubbles, but it\\nstill places J-immediate extraction and a 32-bit PC-relative target adder\\non every fetch-to-PC feedback path. Conditional branches already redirect\\nfrom decode without a bubble when their result is safe to capture.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current JAL-only predictor avoids direct-JAL recovery bubbles, but it\\nstill places J-immediate extraction and a 32-bit PC-relative target adder\\non every fetch-to-PC feedback path. Conditional branches already redirect\\nfrom decode without a bubble when their result is safe to capture.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace only the fetch-stage direct-JAL target prediction with a verified\\ndecode-stage JAL redirect. Fetch remains sequential, while a legal JAL\\nwhose target is instruction aligned steers the PC on the same edge that\\ncaptures it into ID/EX. Carry a one-bit captured-resolution marker so EX\\nrecomputes the architectural successor for RVFI but never performs a\\nduplicate redirect. Misaligned JAL targets, older EX recovery, stalls, and\\nflushes retain their existing EX-stage behavior. This removes the JAL\\nimmediate adder and selection mux from the Fmax-critical fetch loop without\\nadding return prediction, branch-table state, or decode operand bypassing.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"chan\n35:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-004-r12s0\", \"title\": \"Share execute control target adders\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current EX control logic independently builds the same PC-relative\\ntarget for branches and direct JALs, and builds a second rs1-plus-immediate\\nadder for JALR even though its ALU operation is already ADD. These duplicate\\narithmetic cones consume LUTs and routing at the EX/MEM timing endpoint.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current EX control logic independently builds the same PC-relative\\ntarget for branches and direct JALs, and builds a second rs1-plus-immediate\\nadder for JALR even though its ALU operation is already ADD. These duplicate\\narithmetic cones consume LUTs and routing at the EX/MEM timing endpoint.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Refactor EX control-target generation to compute PC+4 and PC+immediate once\\nper instruction, use the latter for both branch and direct-JAL resolution,\\nand derive the JALR sum from the existing ALU result. This removes duplicate\\n32-bit adders without adding state, changing any redirect timing, or\\nchanging the carried RVFI data, so CoreMark IPC should remain unchanged\\nwhile Tang Nano placement and Fmax improve.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Share PC+4 and PC-relative target arithmetic across branch and JAL control paths, and reuse the ADD-class ALU result for the JALR target before bit-zero clearing.\"}, {\"file\": \"\n36:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-005-r12s1\", \"title\": \"Registered fetch decode boundary\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current IF/ID payload is combinational, so an instruction-memory read\\ncan feed decoder/control logic and then steer the next PC in the same clock\\ninterval. The earlier two-entry queue reduced stall cycles but its ready,\\nflush, and occupancy logic reduced Fmax more than that IPC benefit repaid.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current IF/ID payload is combinational, so an instruction-memory read\\ncan feed decoder/control logic and then steer the next PC in the same clock\\ninterval. The earlier two-entry queue reduced stall cycles but its ready,\\nflush, and occupancy logic reduced Fmax more than that IPC benefit repaid.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the combinational IF/ID handoff with one registered, valid-tagged\\nfetch buffer that is consumed every cycle by decode. This deliberately\\navoids a queue, prefetch policy, or predictor lookup: when the buffer is\\nconsumed and imem is ready, fetch refills it in the same edge. Decode-stage\\nbranch and direct-JAL resolution remains intact, with redirects invalidating\\nthe younger refill and winning over all fetch enables. Breaking the\\nimem-to-decode-to-PC feedback path should raise Tang Nano Fmax enough to\\noffset the extra recovery slot on control transfers while preserving\\nsingle-retirement RVFI ordering and all bus-stall behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"},\n37:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-006-r12s2\", \"title\": \"Elide sequential predictor metadata\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core intentionally predicts only PC+4 in fetch, while\\nlegal conditional branches and direct JALs steer from ID. Nevertheless it\\ntransports a 32-bit predicted-next-PC through IF/ID and ID/EX solely to\\nrepresent that invariant sequential successor, adding wide routing and\\nregister load to the control path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core intentionally predicts only PC+4 in fetch, while\\nlegal conditional branches and direct JALs steer from ID. Nevertheless it\\ntransports a 32-bit predicted-next-PC through IF/ID and ID/EX solely to\\nrepresent that invariant sequential successor, adding wide routing and\\nregister load to the control path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Make the sequential predictor implicit rather than carried metadata.\\nRemove predicted_next_pc from the fetch and ID/EX bundles and eliminate\\nthe standalone predictor wiring. IF advances by its local PC+4 default;\\nID determines whether its already-computed branch/JAL successor differs\\nfrom local PC+4, and EX uses the same local comparison only for unresolved\\nJALR recovery. The existing control_resolved_id marker continues to\\nsuppress duplicate EX redirects. This keeps the proven no-table,\\nno-target-adder fetch policy, but removes a redundant 32-bit pipeline\\npayload and its fanout without weakening alignment, trap, or RVFI behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\":\n38:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-004-r13s0\", \"title\": \"Validated load-use source qualification\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The load-use interlock currently compares a load destination with both\\narbitrary source-field bit slices of every following instruction. CoreMark\\ncontains many immediate, U-type, and direct-JAL instructions whose encoded\\nrs2 or both source fields are not read, so aliases create avoidable bubbles\\nduring the measured loop.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The load-use interlock currently compares a load destination with both\\narbitrary source-field bit slices of every following instruction. CoreMark\\ncontains many immediate, U-type, and direct-JAL instructions whose encoded\\nrs2 or both source fields are not read, so aliases create avoidable bubbles\\nduring the measured loop.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add decoder-produced rs1/rs2-read qualifiers that default conservatively to\\nasserted and are cleared only inside the same fully validated legal decode\\narms that clear is_illegal. Gate each load-use comparison with its matching\\nqualifier. This preserves the existing conservative dependency behavior for\\nevery reserved or illegal encoding (the failure mode of prior qualifier\\nattempts), while removing bubbles only for proven source-free fields of\\nlegal OP-IMM, LOAD, JALR, LUI, AUIPC, FENCE, EBREAK, and JAL instructions.\\nNo forwarding, bus timing, or retirement behavior changes; higher useful IPC\\nshould improve CoreMark fitness without lengthening fetch \n39:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-005-r13s1\", \"title\": \"Formal-safe bounded divider split\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The real combinational divide/remainder cone remains in every ordinary EX\\ntiming path even though CoreMark executes divide operations infrequently in\\nits timed loop. Earlier iterative-divider attempts reduced that cone but\\nmade the ALTOPS formal instruction checks unable to observe a retirement\\ninside their bounded window.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The real combinational divide/remainder cone remains in every ordinary EX\\ntiming path even though CoreMark executes divide operations infrequently in\\nits timed loop. Earlier iterative-divider attempts reduced that cone but\\nmade the ALTOPS formal instruction checks unable to observe a retirement\\ninside their bounded window.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split the divider topology by the existing RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS boundary.\\nIn formal builds, DIV/REM retain the current single-cycle ALTOPS result in\\nEX so the bounded RVFI instruction and liveness properties see their normal\\nretirement timing. In FPGA and cosimulation builds, route only real\\nDIV/DIVU/REM/REMU instructions through a fixed four-cycle radix-8 divider\\nthat holds younger issue while older MEM/WB work drains, then injects one\\nordinary EX/MEM result. This removes the large real divider from the common\\nEX cone without changing the architectural RV32M result, RVFI order, or\\nbus contract; the rare divide latency should be outweighed by higher F\n40:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-006-r13s2\", \"title\": \"Decode-stage indirect target cache\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current core resolves direct control flow in decode, but every JALR\\nstill waits for EX and flushes younger sequential fetches. Earlier return\\npredictors lost because their RAS, bypass, and target-generation logic was\\ntoo costly; a trained target lookup can avoid those expensive components.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current core resolves direct control flow in decode, but every JALR\\nstill waits for EX and flushes younger sequential fetches. Earlier return\\npredictors lost because their RAS, bypass, and target-generation logic was\\ntoo costly; a trained target lookup can avoid those expensive components.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny PC-indexed indirect-target cache that is looked up only in ID,\\nnot on the fetch-to-PC path. A valid cache hit on a legal JALR speculatively\\nredirects via the existing ID redirect port and substitutes the cached\\nsuccessor into the already-carried predicted-next-PC field. EX continues to\\ncompute the architectural JALR target, trains only resolved aligned JALRs,\\nand recovers normally on a stale target. This removes recurring JALR\\nrecovery bubbles when targets are stable while avoiding an RAS, source\\nbypass network, or target adder in the decode fast path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a very sma\n41:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-004-r14s0\", \"title\": \"Right-size independent RV32 multipliers\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The ALU builds three multiply products on every cycle after explicitly\\nextending both RV32 operands to 64 bits. This can infer oversized multiplier\\ncones and unnecessarily consume LUT/routing resources near the EX endpoint.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The ALU builds three multiply products on every cycle after explicitly\\nextending both RV32 operands to 64 bits. This can infer oversized multiplier\\ncones and unnecessarily consume LUT/routing resources near the EX endpoint.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep the current independent signed-signed, unsigned-unsigned, and\\nsigned-unsigned product topology, but express each product with native\\n32-bit operands and a 64-bit result. Unlike the prior single shared\\nmultiplier experiment, this avoids an operation-selected operand-extension\\nmux and its control fanout on the multiplier inputs. The narrower fixed\\nproducts preserve every MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU bit result while reducing\\narithmetic width and placement pressure, which should raise Fmax without\\nchanging pipeline timing or CoreMark IPC.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the explicit 64-bit operand extensions feeding mul_ss, mul_uu, and mul_su with correctly typed native RV32 signed/unsigned operands while retaining separate fixed product cones, result-hal\n42:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-005-r14s1\", \"title\": \"In-order memory lookahead queue\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The CoreMark cosimulation injects approximately 22% dmem backpressure,\\nwhile the current EX/MEM register freezes fetch, decode, and execute for\\nevery pending load or store. CoreMark is load/store dense, so this converts\\nmany independent ALU and control slots into bubbles even though retirement\\nneed not be reordered.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The CoreMark cosimulation injects approximately 22% dmem backpressure,\\nwhile the current EX/MEM register freezes fetch, decode, and execute for\\nevery pending load or store. CoreMark is load/store dense, so this converts\\nmany independent ALU and control slots into bubbles even though retirement\\nneed not be reordered.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the single EX/MEM holding point with a compact two-entry in-order\\nexecution-to-memory queue. The head alone owns the dmem request and waits\\nfor its response, while one younger independent instruction may enter the\\ntail and retain its completed EX result. Commit remains strictly head-first\\ninto MEM/WB, so RVFI retirement order and memory visibility remain exactly\\nin order. A conservative pending-load scoreboard stalls only instructions\\nthat read an unresolved load destination; forwarding from completed queued\\nALU entries preserves ordinary dependent ALU chains. This hides isolated\\ndmem stalls without placing predictor lookup, instruction fetch, or ALU\\narithm\n43:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-006-r14s2\", \"title\": \"Load-dependent branch direction rescue\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Operand-ready conditional branches and aligned direct JALs already steer in\\ndecode, leaving load-dependent conditional branches as the meaningful\\nrecurring control-flow bubble. The current sequential policy stalls one\\ncycle before such a branch can be resolved in decode.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Operand-ready conditional branches and aligned direct JALs already steer in\\ndecode, leaving load-dependent conditional branches as the meaningful\\nrecurring control-flow bubble. The current sequential policy stalls one\\ncycle before such a branch can be resolved in decode.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny weakly-not-taken, PC-indexed two-bit direction table that is\\nconsulted only when a legal conditional branch is blocked by a matching\\nload in EX. On a taken prediction, capture that branch into ID/EX without\\nthe normal load-use bubble and steer fetch to its already-formed B-immediate\\ntarget; EX forwards the completed load, resolves the architectural outcome,\\ntrains the table, and recovers on a miss. All operand-ready branches retain\\ntheir proven decode-resolution path, and fetch remains the existing simple\\nsequential predictor, so the BHT never extends the fetch-to-PC timing cone.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small reset-to-weakly-\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
