red counted-loop back edge pays a recovery before the table\\nlearns its overwhelmingly taken steady-state direction. CoreMark repeatedly\\nenters compact loops, while the prior BTFNT fallback showed that adding\\nlookup logic to address this warm-up cost hurts Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current BHT initializes every counter as weakly not taken, so each\\nnewly encountered counted-loop back edge pays a recovery before the table\\nlearns its overwhelmingly taken steady-state direction. CoreMark repeatedly\\nenters compact loops, while the prior BTFNT fallback showed that adding\\nlookup logic to address this warm-up cost hurts Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Initialize the existing sixteen two-bit BHT counters to weakly taken\\n(2'b10) instead of weakly not taken (2'b01). This removes the first-taken\\nmisprediction for cold loop back edges with no new state, muxing, PC hash,\\nor fetch-path logic. A first not-taken forward branch still trains the same\\ncounter to weakly not taken after one recovery, retaining normal dynamic\\nbehavior and existing decode-stage training semantics.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Change BHT reset initialization to weakly taken while retaining the current direct PC index, saturating updates, JAL handling, and decode-stage training interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_branch_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add reset-state tests for a cold taken branch and a cold not-taken branch, then verify counter transitions, saturation, and unchanged direct JAL prediction.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Change BHT reset initialization to weakly taken while retaining the current direct PC index, saturating updates, JAL handling, and decode-stage training interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_branch_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add reset-state tests for a cold taken branch and a cold not-taken branch, then verify counter transitions, saturation, and unchanged direct JAL prediction.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 338.8, \"delta_pct\": -1.9, \"vs_baseline\": 19.79, \"fmax_mhz\": 140.55, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4148503, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10700, \"ff\": 1927, \"seeds\": [137.44, 142.25, 140.55], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T16:45:01.518248Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 6, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r6s2 (regression, -1.90%): With a two-bit BHT already in the fetch path, weakly-taken reset harms CoreMark more through cold forward/not-taken branches and aliases than it saves on first counted-loop back edges, so the weakly-not-taken cold bias is the better default.\"}\n21:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r7s1\", \"title\": \"Forwarded decode-stage JALR recovery\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current predictor already removes direct-JAL and steady-state branch\\nbubbles, but every JALR still waits for EX to repair the sequential fetch\\npath. CoreMark's calls and returns make that recurring recovery cost a\\nmeaningful remaining front-end loss.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current predictor already removes direct-JAL and steady-state branch\\nbubbles, but every JALR still waits for EX to repair the sequential fetch\\npath. CoreMark's calls and returns make that recurring recovery cost a\\nmeaningful remaining front-end loss.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Resolve legal, aligned JALR targets in ID using the existing EX/MEM/WB\\nbypass priority, then redirect fetch one stage earlier. Crucially, latch\\nthose same selected source operands into ID/EX so EX, register writeback,\\nand RVFI rs1_rdata retain the architecturally current value; the previous\\ndecode-JALR attempt redirected using a forwarded value while retaining a\\nstale operand, which broke the formal register check. Load-dependent and\\nmisaligned JALRs remain on the precise EX path, and a resolved-in-ID flag\\nsuppresses only the duplicate EX redirect.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Generalize the ID/EX branch-resolved marker into a control-resolved-in-ID marker while retaining the existing compact payload.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Apply the established EX/MEM/WB bypass priority to legal JALR target calculation and latch the selected operands, not raw regfile values, into ID/EX for JALR.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the control-resolved-in-ID marker to suppress only duplicate JALR recovery while preserving link writeback, misalignment trapping, and RVFI operand reporting.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Route the validated decode-stage JALR redirect through the existing redirect-priority path without adding predictor lookup logic.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Preserve load-use and dmem-stall precedence so a JALR dependent on a load remains unresolved until its operand is forwardable.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add independent, ALU-forwarded, load-dependent, and misaligned JALR cases that check redirect depth, link writeback, RVFI rs1 data, register state, and retirement order.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Generalize the ID/EX branch-resolved marker into a control-resolved-in-ID marker while retaining the existing compact payload.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Apply the established EX/MEM/WB bypass priority to legal JALR target calculation and latch the selected operan